(window.__PQ=window.__PQ||{})["04-kubernetes"]={"slug":"04-kubernetes","title":"Kubernetes","accent":"#a78bfa","sections":[{"id":"s01-control-plane","title":"Control plane and etcd","c":[{"id":"s01-control-plane-c1","t":"The cluster at a glance","p":"A cluster is a control plane that makes decisions and worker nodes that run your containers. You talk only to the control plane, through the API server.","k":[["Which component do kubectl and the kubelet both talk to?","The kube-apiserver. It is the only component that reads and writes etcd."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-control-plane-c2","t":"The API server","p":"The kube-apiserver is a stateless HTTPS server that exposes every Kubernetes object as a REST resource. It is the single gateway to the cluster state.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-control-plane-c3","t":"etcd, the state store","p":"etcd is a small, strongly consistent key-value database. Every object you create, such as a Deployment or Secret, is a key in etcd with its serialised value.","k":[["Why do slow disks hurt the whole cluster?","etcd fsyncs every write. Slow fsync delays commits, heartbeats are missed, leaders change, and the API server times out."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-control-plane-c4","t":"Raft and quorum","p":"Raft is how etcd members agree. One member is the leader, writes go through it, and a write counts only after a majority has stored it.","k":[["How many failures does a 5-member etcd tolerate?","Two. Quorum is 3, so 5 - 3 = 2."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-control-plane-c5","t":"kube-scheduler","p":"The scheduler watches for Pods with no node assigned and picks the best node for each one.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-control-plane-c6","t":"Controllers and reconcile loops","p":"A controller is a loop that compares desired state (the spec) with observed state (the status) and acts to close the gap. It then repeats forever.","k":[["What does level driven mean?","The controller reacts to the current state difference, not to individual events, so lost or duplicated events do not matter."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-control-plane-c7","t":"Cloud controller manager and managed control planes","p":"The cloud-controller-manager holds the code that talks to a cloud provider API: create load balancers, find node metadata and remove nodes whose VM is gone.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s02-api-request-flow","title":"API request flow","c":[{"id":"s02-api-request-flow-c1","t":"Resources, groups and versions","p":"Every object type has a group, a version and a kind, for example apps/v1 and Deployment. The REST path is built from them.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-api-request-flow-c2","t":"The request pipeline","p":"A write passes through fixed stages: authentication, authorisation, mutating admission, schema validation, validating admission, and finally storage in etcd.","k":[["A request fails with \"forbidden\". Which stage rejected it?","Authorisation. A 401 means authentication failed; a webhook denial mentions \"admission webhook\" or a policy name."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-api-request-flow-c3","t":"Authentication","p":"The API server decides who you are from a credential on the request. It has no user database of its own for people.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-api-request-flow-c4","t":"Authorisation","p":"After identity is known, authorisers decide whether that identity may perform a verb (get, create, delete) on a resource in a namespace.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-api-request-flow-c5","t":"Admission control","p":"Admission controllers are plug-ins that inspect or change a request after it is authorised and before it is stored. They apply to create, update and delete, but not to reads.","k":[["Can a validating webhook modify an object?","No. Only mutating admission can change it; validating can only accept or deny."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-api-request-flow-c6","t":"Validation, persistence and optimistic concurrency","p":"Every object carries a resourceVersion. An update succeeds only if the version you send is still the current one.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-api-request-flow-c7","t":"Watches and informers","p":"A watch is a long lived HTTP request that streams changes to objects. Controllers use it instead of polling.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-api-request-flow-c8","t":"Server-side apply","p":"Server-side apply (SSA) lets the API server track which actor owns which field and merge changes from several actors safely.","k":[["Why use SSA with an HPA?","The HPA owns `spec.replicas`; if Git does not set it, SSA leaves the HPA value alone instead of resetting it on every sync."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s03-node-components","title":"Nodes, kubelet and the runtime interfaces","c":[{"id":"s03-node-components-c1","t":"The kubelet","p":"The kubelet is the agent on every node. It watches the API server for Pods assigned to its node and makes the container runtime match them.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-node-components-c2","t":"CRI and the container runtime","p":"The Container Runtime Interface is a gRPC API between the kubelet and a runtime. containerd and CRI-O implement it. Docker Engine support (dockershim) was removed in 1.24.","k":[["Why does every Pod have a pause container?","It owns the shared network namespace so app containers can restart without losing the Pod IP."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-node-components-c3","t":"CNI, the Pod network plug-in","p":"The Container Network Interface is how the runtime asks a plug-in to give a new Pod sandbox an IP and wire it into the cluster network.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-node-components-c4","t":"CSI, the storage plug-in","p":"The Container Storage Interface lets storage vendors ship a driver as ordinary Pods, instead of code compiled into Kubernetes.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-node-components-c5","t":"Node lifecycle and health","p":"Each kubelet renews a Lease object every few seconds and reports node conditions such as Ready, MemoryPressure and DiskPressure.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-node-components-c6","t":"Static Pods, kube-proxy and node add-ons","p":"Some Pods are not created through the API. Static Pods are defined by manifest files the kubelet reads from disk. DaemonSets run one agent per node through the API.","k":[["Where does a kubelet find static Pod manifests?","In the directory set by `staticPodPath` in its config, commonly `/etc/kubernetes/manifests`."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s04-objects-namespaces-extensibility","title":"Objects, namespaces and extending the API","c":[{"id":"s04-objects-namespaces-extensibility-c1","t":"The anatomy of an object","p":"Every object has apiVersion, kind, metadata, usually a spec (what you want) and a status (what the controllers observed).","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-objects-namespaces-extensibility-c2","t":"Labels, selectors and annotations","p":"Labels are key-value tags used to group objects. Selectors pick objects by those tags. Annotations are free-form metadata that nothing selects on.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-objects-namespaces-extensibility-c3","t":"Namespaces","p":"Namespaces split one cluster into virtual sub-clusters for names, permissions and quotas.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-objects-namespaces-extensibility-c4","t":"Owners, garbage collection and finalizers","p":"ownerReferences link a child to its parent. When the parent goes, the garbage collector removes children. Finalizers hold deletion until cleanup is done.","k":[["What does `--cascade=orphan` do?","Deletes the parent but leaves the children running without an owner."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-objects-namespaces-extensibility-c5","t":"Custom resources and operators","p":"A CustomResourceDefinition (CRD) adds a new kind to the API. A controller for it, often called an operator, reconciles it like any built-in.","k":[["Does a CRD alone do anything?","No. It only stores validated data. Behaviour needs a controller that watches the resource."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-objects-namespaces-extensibility-c6","t":"API Priority and Fairness, events and audit","p":"The API server protects itself from overload by queueing requests per client class, records Events about object changes, and can write an audit log of every request.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s05-pods","title":"Pods, containers and lifecycle","c":[{"id":"s05-pods-c1","t":"What a Pod is","p":"A Pod is one or more containers that share a network identity (one IP), storage volumes and a lifecycle. They are scheduled together onto one node.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-pods-c2","t":"Init containers and sidecars","p":"Init containers run one after another to completion before the app starts. Native sidecars are init containers marked restartPolicy: Always that keep running beside the app.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-pods-c3","t":"Pod phases and container states","p":"A Pod has a phase (Pending, Running, Succeeded, Failed, Unknown) and each container has a state (Waiting, Running, Terminated) with a reason.","k":[["Exit code 137 means what?","The process was killed by SIGKILL, most often by the OOM killer (check for reason OOMKilled) or after the grace period ended."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-pods-c4","t":"Probes","p":"Probes are health checks the kubelet runs against a container. Startup says it has booted, readiness says it can take traffic, liveness says it is not stuck.","k":[["The app needs 90 seconds to start. Which probe handles that?","A startup probe, for example periodSeconds 5 with failureThreshold 30 allows 150 seconds before liveness takes over."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-pods-c5","t":"Graceful termination","p":"When a Pod is deleted, Kubernetes sends SIGTERM, waits up to terminationGracePeriodSeconds (default 30), then sends SIGKILL.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-pods-c6","t":"Restart policy, CrashLoopBackOff and image pulls","p":"restartPolicy decides what the kubelet does when a container exits. Repeated failures cause growing waits, shown as CrashLoopBackOff.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s06-deployments","title":"ReplicaSets, Deployments and rollouts","c":[{"id":"s06-deployments-c1","t":"ReplicaSets","p":"A ReplicaSet keeps a fixed number of identical Pods running. If a Pod disappears it creates another.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-deployments-c2","t":"Deployments","p":"A Deployment manages ReplicaSets to move your app from one version to another in a controlled way.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-deployments-c3","t":"Rolling update tuning","p":"maxSurge is how many extra Pods may exist during a rollout. maxUnavailable is how many may be missing. Both accept a number or a percentage.","k":[["Why might a rollout with a broken image never finish yet cause no outage?","New Pods fail readiness so they never count as available, and with maxUnavailable limits the old Pods stay. The rollout stalls at the progress deadline."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-deployments-c4","t":"Blue green and canary releases","p":"Blue green runs two full versions and switches traffic at once. A canary sends a small share of traffic to the new version first.","k":[],"o":[["Rolling update","stateless apps with compatible versions","breaking schema or API changes"],["Blue green","instant switch and instant rollback","capacity is tight"],["Canary","risky changes you want to measure","you have no metrics to judge success"]]},{"id":"s06-deployments-c5","t":"Scaling, labels and Deployment pitfalls","p":"kubectl scale changes replicas. Most Deployment trouble comes from a few repeating mistakes.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-deployments-c6","t":"Progress, availability and PodDisruptionBudgets","p":"A Deployment reports conditions Progressing and Available. A PodDisruptionBudget limits how many Pods voluntary actions such as drains may remove.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s07-statefulset-daemonset","title":"StatefulSets and DaemonSets","c":[{"id":"s07-statefulset-daemonset-c1","t":"Why StatefulSets exist","p":"A StatefulSet runs Pods with a stable name, a stable network identity and their own persistent disk, which survive rescheduling.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-statefulset-daemonset-c2","t":"StatefulSet updates and failure behaviour","p":"StatefulSets update Pods one at a time from the highest ordinal down, waiting for each to be Ready.","k":[["What does `partition: 2` on a 3-replica StatefulSet do?","Only db-2 receives the new template; db-0 and db-1 stay on the old revision."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-statefulset-daemonset-c3","t":"DaemonSets","p":"A DaemonSet runs one Pod on every node, or on every node that matches a selector.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-statefulset-daemonset-c4","t":"Choosing the right workload controller","p":"Each controller models a different promise about identity, ordering and completion.","k":[],"o":[["Deployment","web apps, APIs, workers","you need a stable hostname or own disk"],["StatefulSet","databases, brokers, quorum systems","the app is stateless"],["DaemonSet","log, metric, network, storage agents","you want N replicas anywhere"],["Job","batch, migrations","it must run forever"]]},{"id":"s07-statefulset-daemonset-c5","t":"Operating StatefulSets safely","p":"Most StatefulSet incidents are storage related: a Pod bound to a disk in one zone, or a volume that cannot attach.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-statefulset-daemonset-c6","t":"Headless Services and Pod DNS","p":"A headless Service has no virtual IP. Its DNS name returns the individual Pod IPs, and each StatefulSet Pod gets its own record.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s08-jobs-cronjobs","title":"Jobs and CronJobs","c":[{"id":"s08-jobs-cronjobs-c1","t":"Jobs","p":"A Job creates Pods and keeps retrying until a set number of them finish successfully.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-jobs-cronjobs-c2","t":"Parallel and indexed Jobs","p":"Jobs can split work across many Pods, either with a shared queue or with each Pod getting a fixed index.","k":[["How does a Pod know which slice to process?","In Indexed mode it reads the `JOB_COMPLETION_INDEX` environment variable."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-jobs-cronjobs-c3","t":"CronJobs","p":"A CronJob creates a Job on a schedule written in cron syntax.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-jobs-cronjobs-c4","t":"Operating Jobs","p":"Jobs are easy to write and hard to observe. Know the commands that show why they stall.","k":[["Why must Job code be idempotent?","Kubernetes guarantees at least once, not exactly once; retries or overlapping runs can repeat work."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-jobs-cronjobs-c5","t":"Batch at scale with queues","p":"For large batch loads, Kubernetes Jobs combine with queueing and quotas so one team cannot starve the rest.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s09-services","title":"Services and kube-proxy","c":[{"id":"s09-services-c1","t":"The Kubernetes network model","p":"Every Pod gets its own IP. Any Pod can reach any other Pod directly without NAT, and nodes can reach all Pods.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-services-c2","t":"Service types","p":"A Service picks Pods with a label selector and exposes them in one of four ways: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer or ExternalName.","k":[["Which port is `port` and which is `targetPort`?","`port` is what clients call on the Service; `targetPort` is the container port receiving traffic."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-services-c3","t":"How kube-proxy implements a Service","p":"kube-proxy runs on each node and programs the kernel so that traffic to a Service IP is redirected to one of its Pod IPs. It does not carry traffic itself in the common modes.","k":[["Why do conntrack table limits matter?","NAT'd connections are tracked per flow; a full table drops new connections, often seen as random timeouts under load."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-services-c4","t":"EndpointSlices","p":"An EndpointSlice lists the IPs, ports and readiness of Pods behind a Service. The old single Endpoints object is replaced by sharded slices.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-services-c5","t":"Traffic policies and session behaviour","p":"Traffic policies control which endpoints a Service may use: any, only local to the node, or preferring the same zone.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-services-c6","t":"Debugging Service connectivity","p":"A methodical order finds a broken Service quickly: Pod, selector, endpoints, port, DNS, policy.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s10-dns","title":"DNS and service discovery","c":[{"id":"s10-dns-c1","t":"Cluster DNS names","p":"CoreDNS answers names for Services and Pods inside the cluster domain, cluster.local by default.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-dns-c2","t":"ndots and the search path","p":"options ndots:5 means any name with fewer than five dots is first tried with each search domain appended, before being tried as is.","k":[["Why is `api.example.com.` with a trailing dot faster?","It is a fully qualified name, so the resolver skips the search list."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-dns-c3","t":"CoreDNS configuration","p":"CoreDNS is a plug-in based DNS server configured by a Corefile in a ConfigMap. Each plug-in does one job: cluster zone, cache, forward, health.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-dns-c4","t":"DNS scale and NodeLocal DNSCache","p":"Heavy lookup traffic can overload CoreDNS and conntrack. NodeLocal DNSCache runs a caching agent on every node.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-dns-c5","t":"Debugging DNS","p":"DNS failures show as timeouts, NXDOMAIN or slow requests. Separate the layers: Pod resolver, CoreDNS, upstream.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-dns-c6","t":"ExternalDNS and public names","p":"ExternalDNS watches Services, Ingresses and Gateway routes and creates matching records in Route 53, Cloud DNS or similar.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s11-ingress-gateway","title":"Ingress and Gateway API","c":[{"id":"s11-ingress-gateway-c1","t":"Why L7 routing","p":"A LoadBalancer Service gives one external IP per Service at layer 4. Layer 7 routing shares one entry point among many apps by reading the HTTP host and path.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-ingress-gateway-c2","t":"Ingress","p":"An Ingress is a single object with host and path rules pointing to Services, handled by the controller named in ingressClassName.","k":[["Why can the same Ingress YAML behave differently on two clusters?","Annotations and defaults belong to the controller, and each controller interprets them differently."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-ingress-gateway-c3","t":"Ingress controllers","p":"The controller watches Ingress objects and configures a proxy. Some run a proxy inside the cluster; some program a cloud load balancer.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-ingress-gateway-c4","t":"Gateway API model","p":"Gateway API splits routing into role-based objects: GatewayClass (the implementation), Gateway (listeners, owned by the platform team) and routes (owned by app teams).","k":[["Which object lets a team in namespace shop use a Gateway in namespace infra?","The Gateway's `allowedRoutes` must permit that namespace; the HTTPRoute then names the Gateway in `parentRefs`."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-ingress-gateway-c5","t":"Ingress versus Gateway API","p":"Ingress is frozen: still supported, but no new features. Gateway API is the successor and where new routing capabilities land.","k":[],"o":[["Ingress","simple HTTP routing on an existing controller","you need weights, header routing or shared Gateways"],["Gateway API","multi-team platforms, canaries, non-HTTP routes","your controller lacks conformance for the features you need"]]},{"id":"s11-ingress-gateway-c6","t":"TLS and cert-manager","p":"cert-manager requests certificates from an issuer (Let's Encrypt, a private CA, Vault, AWS PCA) and keeps them renewed as Kubernetes Secrets.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-ingress-gateway-c7","t":"Kyma APIRule and Istio gateways","p":"Kyma exposes workloads with its own APIRule custom resource, which creates Istio VirtualService and optional authentication rules behind the Kyma gateway.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s12-network-cni-egress","title":"CNI choices, load balancers and egress","c":[{"id":"s12-network-cni-egress-c1","t":"Pod networking models","p":"CNI plug-ins connect Pods in one of three ways: an overlay tunnel, plain routing, or addresses taken directly from the cloud network.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-network-cni-egress-c2","t":"Cloud load balancers","p":"A type: LoadBalancer Service or an Ingress makes a controller create a cloud load balancer. How it reaches Pods depends on the target type.","k":[["Why use IP targets rather than instance targets with the VPC CNI?","Traffic reaches Pods directly: one less hop, true source IP kept, and no dependence on NodePort and kube-proxy."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-network-cni-egress-c3","t":"Egress and NAT","p":"Egress is traffic leaving the cluster. By default Pods reach the internet through the node's route, usually a NAT gateway, so all Pods share a few source IPs.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-network-cni-egress-c4","t":"MTU, hairpin and common network faults","p":"Some failures only appear with large packets, specific paths or when a Pod calls its own Service.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-network-cni-egress-c5","t":"Service mesh and network observability preview","p":"A mesh adds a proxy to each Pod (or node) to supply mTLS, retries and metrics without app changes. eBPF tools show flows without a mesh.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s13-configmaps-secrets","title":"ConfigMaps and Secrets","c":[{"id":"s13-configmaps-secrets-c1","t":"ConfigMaps","p":"A ConfigMap stores non-secret configuration as key-value pairs or whole files.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-configmaps-secrets-c2","t":"How config updates reach a Pod","p":"Environment variables are fixed at container start. Files from a ConfigMap volume are refreshed by the kubelet, but only when you do not use subPath.","k":[["You changed a ConfigMap used via envFrom and nothing changed. Why?","Environment variables are read when the container starts; restart the Pods."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-configmaps-secrets-c3","t":"Secrets","p":"A Secret holds sensitive values such as passwords, tokens and keys. It is used like a ConfigMap but with tighter handling.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-configmaps-secrets-c4","t":"Protecting Secrets in the cluster","p":"Secrets need encryption at rest in etcd, tight RBAC and audit.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-configmaps-secrets-c5","t":"External Secrets Operator","p":"External Secrets Operator (ESO) reads secrets from a cloud store such as AWS Secrets Manager, Azure Key Vault or HashiCorp Vault and creates ordinary Kubernetes Secrets from them.","k":[["Why is IRSA or Pod Identity better than an access key Secret for ESO?","The pod gets short-lived credentials tied to a role, so no long-lived key is stored in the cluster."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-configmaps-secrets-c6","t":"Secrets Store CSI driver, Sealed Secrets and SOPS","p":"Three other patterns: mount secrets straight from a store as files, encrypt secrets so they can live in Git, or decrypt them in the pipeline.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s14-config-patterns","title":"Pod configuration patterns","c":[{"id":"s14-config-patterns-c1","t":"The Downward API","p":"The Downward API lets a container read facts about its own Pod, such as its name, namespace, IP, labels and resource limits, without calling the API server.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-config-patterns-c2","t":"Projected volumes and service account tokens","p":"A projected volume combines several sources (ServiceAccount token, ConfigMap, Secret, Downward API) into one directory. Bound service account tokens are short lived and audience scoped.","k":[["Why prefer a projected token to a token Secret?","It expires, is rotated automatically, is bound to the Pod and is scoped to an audience."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-config-patterns-c3","t":"ResourceQuota","p":"A ResourceQuota caps the total resources and object counts a namespace may use.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-config-patterns-c4","t":"LimitRange","p":"A LimitRange sets default, minimum and maximum resources for containers in a namespace.","k":[["A namespace has a CPU quota but a Pod without CPU requests is rejected. Fix?","Add requests to the Pod or a LimitRange with `defaultRequest`."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-config-patterns-c5","t":"Declarative configuration and immutable infrastructure","p":"Treat every manifest as code in Git, applied by a controller, and avoid hand edits to live objects.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s15-storage-pv-pvc","title":"Volumes, PersistentVolumes and StorageClasses","c":[{"id":"s15-storage-pv-pvc-c1","t":"Volume types","p":"A volume is a directory made available to containers in a Pod. Its lifetime and backing storage depend on the type.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-storage-pv-pvc-c2","t":"PersistentVolume and PersistentVolumeClaim","p":"A PV is a piece of storage in the cluster. A PVC is a user's request for storage of a size and access mode. Kubernetes binds one PVC to one matching PV.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-storage-pv-pvc-c3","t":"Access modes","p":"Access modes say how many nodes may use a volume and whether they may write: ReadWriteOnce, ReadOnlyMany, ReadWriteMany, ReadWriteOncePod.","k":[["Why does a Deployment with an RWO PVC fail rolling updates across nodes?","The new Pod lands on another node while the volume is still attached to the old one: Multi-Attach error. Use Recreate, a StatefulSet, or RWX."]],"o":[["RWOP","a database that must have a single writer","several replicas must share files"],["RWO","block disk for one Pod or one node","replicas on different nodes need the same data"],["RWX","shared uploads or content","you need low latency block performance"]]},{"id":"s15-storage-pv-pvc-c4","t":"StorageClasses and dynamic provisioning","p":"A StorageClass describes a kind of storage (type, IOPS, encryption) and the CSI driver that creates it on demand when a PVC names the class.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-storage-pv-pvc-c5","t":"Reclaim, resize and static PVs","p":"What happens to data when a claim goes away, how to grow a disk, and how to adopt an existing one.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-storage-pv-pvc-c6","t":"Debugging storage","p":"Storage failures show up as Pods stuck in ContainerCreating or PVCs stuck in Pending.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s16-csi-snapshots-backup","title":"Snapshots, cloning and backup","c":[{"id":"s16-csi-snapshots-backup-c1","t":"VolumeSnapshots","p":"A VolumeSnapshot is a point-in-time copy of a PVC, created by the CSI driver using the storage system's snapshot feature.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-csi-snapshots-backup-c2","t":"Restore and clone","p":"You restore by creating a new PVC whose dataSource is a snapshot. A clone does the same from another PVC.","k":[["Does restoring a snapshot overwrite the original volume?","No. It creates a new volume; you then point the workload at it."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-csi-snapshots-backup-c3","t":"Application consistent backups","p":"A snapshot taken while a database writes may need crash recovery on restore. Quiescing or using native dumps gives clean copies.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-csi-snapshots-backup-c4","t":"Velero","p":"Velero backs up Kubernetes objects and volume data to object storage, and restores them into the same or another cluster.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-csi-snapshots-backup-c5","t":"Ephemeral storage, local volumes and performance","p":"Containers also use node disk through logs, writable layers and emptyDir. Local SSDs and instance stores give speed without durability.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s17-requests-limits-qos","title":"Requests, limits and QoS","c":[{"id":"s17-requests-limits-qos-c1","t":"Requests and limits","p":"A request is what the scheduler reserves for a container. A limit is the ceiling the kernel enforces at run time.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-requests-limits-qos-c2","t":"CPU throttling","p":"A CPU limit is enforced with the CFS quota: in each 100 ms period the container may use only limit x 100 ms of CPU time, then it is paused until the next period.","k":[["Why can a service be throttled at 20 percent average CPU?","Throttling is decided per 100 ms period; short bursts across many threads can exhaust the quota while the average stays low."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-requests-limits-qos-c3","t":"Memory limits and OOM kills","p":"Exceeding the memory limit makes the kernel's OOM killer terminate the container's process. Kubernetes shows OOMKilled and exit code 137.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-requests-limits-qos-c4","t":"QoS classes and eviction order","p":"Kubernetes labels each Pod Guaranteed, Burstable or BestEffort from its requests and limits, and uses it to decide who dies first under pressure.","k":[["A Pod has memory requests equal to limits but only a CPU request. QoS?","Burstable. Guaranteed needs requests equal to limits for both CPU and memory in every container."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-requests-limits-qos-c5","t":"Right-sizing, in-place resize and PriorityClass","p":"Good numbers come from measurement. Newer Kubernetes can resize a running Pod, and PriorityClass says which Pods matter more.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-requests-limits-qos-c6","t":"Node pressure, eviction and allocatable","p":"Each node reserves resources for the OS and kubelet and evicts Pods before the machine runs out of memory or disk.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s18-scheduler","title":"The scheduler in depth","c":[{"id":"s18-scheduler-c1","t":"Scheduling cycle and plugins","p":"For each Pod the scheduler runs a queue, a filter phase, a score phase and a bind phase. Each phase is made of plugins you can enable, disable or weight.","k":[["What does a message listing counts per reason tell you?","Every node was rejected by one of those filters, so the Pod stays Pending until one reason is resolved."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-scheduler-c2","t":"nodeSelector and node affinity","p":"nodeSelector picks nodes by exact labels. Node affinity adds operators, required versus preferred rules and weights.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-scheduler-c3","t":"Pod affinity and anti-affinity","p":"Pod affinity attracts a Pod toward nodes (or zones) that already run certain other Pods. Anti-affinity repels it.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-scheduler-c4","t":"Taints and tolerations","p":"A taint on a node repels Pods. A toleration on a Pod lets it ignore a matching taint. Taints keep unwanted Pods out; they do not attract.","k":[["Does a toleration make a Pod go to the tainted node?","No. It only permits it. Add node affinity or a selector to attract it."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-scheduler-c5","t":"Topology spread constraints","p":"Topology spread constraints keep Pods evenly distributed across zones, nodes or any label domain, within an allowed difference called maxSkew.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-scheduler-c6","t":"PodDisruptionBudgets and node maintenance","p":"Voluntary disruptions (drain, upgrade, autoscaler scale-down) go through the Eviction API, which respects PodDisruptionBudgets. Involuntary ones (crash, hardware) do not.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s19-hpa-vpa-keda","title":"Pod autoscaling with HPA, VPA and KEDA","c":[{"id":"s19-hpa-vpa-keda-c1","t":"Metrics for autoscaling","p":"Autoscalers read metrics through three APIs: resource metrics from metrics-server, custom metrics about Kubernetes objects, and external metrics from outside the cluster.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-hpa-vpa-keda-c2","t":"The Horizontal Pod Autoscaler","p":"The HPA changes replicas of a Deployment or StatefulSet to keep a metric near a target.","k":[["Why does HPA need CPU requests on the Pods?","Utilisation is measured as a percentage of the request; without one there is nothing to compare against."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-hpa-vpa-keda-c3","t":"HPA behaviour and a full manifest","p":"The behavior section controls how fast the HPA may scale up or down and how long it waits before scaling down.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-hpa-vpa-keda-c4","t":"Vertical Pod Autoscaler","p":"The VPA watches real usage and recommends, or applies, better CPU and memory requests for a workload.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-hpa-vpa-keda-c5","t":"KEDA, event-driven scaling","p":"KEDA scales workloads on events such as queue length, Kafka lag, cron schedules or Prometheus queries, including down to zero.","k":[],"o":[["HPA","CPU or request-rate scaling of web services","you need scale to zero or external queues"],["KEDA","queues, streams, schedules, scale to zero","plain CPU scaling is enough"],["VPA (Off mode)","finding correct requests","you want it to restart Pods during peak hours"]]},{"id":"s19-hpa-vpa-keda-c6","t":"Autoscaling pitfalls","p":"Most autoscaling incidents come from slow starts, missing limits on dependencies or fighting controllers.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s20-node-autoscaling","title":"Node autoscaling with Cluster Autoscaler and Karpenter","c":[{"id":"s20-node-autoscaling-c1","t":"What node autoscalers do","p":"A node autoscaler adds nodes when Pods are Pending for lack of capacity and removes nodes that are empty or can be consolidated.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-node-autoscaling-c2","t":"Cluster Autoscaler","p":"Cluster Autoscaler (CA) scales pre-defined node groups (AWS Auto Scaling Groups, Gardener worker pools, managed node groups) up and down.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-node-autoscaling-c3","t":"Karpenter","p":"Karpenter creates nodes directly from the pending Pods' needs, choosing instance types and sizes on the fly, without pre-defined node groups.","k":[["How does Karpenter differ from CA?","It selects instance types from Pod requirements and creates nodes directly, rather than resizing fixed node groups."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-node-autoscaling-c4","t":"Consolidation and disruption safety","p":"Cost optimisation means moving Pods. Make that safe with PDBs, budgets and do-not-disrupt markers.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-node-autoscaling-c5","t":"Capacity planning and cost","p":"Autoscaling finds machines; you still decide the shape of the fleet, how much headroom to hold and where the money goes.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s21-rbac","title":"RBAC and identity for workloads","c":[{"id":"s21-rbac-c1","t":"RBAC building blocks","p":"Four objects: Role (permissions in a namespace), ClusterRole (cluster-wide or reusable permissions), RoleBinding and ClusterRoleBinding (attach a role to subjects).","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-rbac-c2","t":"ClusterRoles, aggregation and built-ins","p":"Kubernetes ships cluster-admin, admin, edit and view. Aggregated ClusterRoles let CRDs add themselves to them with labels.","k":[["Why is `edit` risky?","It can read Secrets, including tokens and passwords used by other workloads in the namespace."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-rbac-c3","t":"ServiceAccounts","p":"A ServiceAccount is the identity of a workload inside Kubernetes. Pods run as one and use it to call the API.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-rbac-c4","t":"Cloud identity for Pods","p":"Pods call AWS, Azure or Google APIs by exchanging their Kubernetes token for short-lived cloud credentials, with no stored keys.","k":[["What does the IAM trust policy for IRSA limit?","Which OIDC subject (namespace and ServiceAccount) can assume the role."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-rbac-c5","t":"Testing and auditing RBAC","p":"You can ask the API server what an identity may do, and use tools to find dangerous or unused permissions.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-rbac-c6","t":"Kubernetes access for people","p":"Humans should reach clusters through your identity provider with short sessions and group-based roles, not shared kubeconfigs with client certificates.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s22-pod-security","title":"Pod security","c":[{"id":"s22-pod-security-c1","t":"securityContext","p":"securityContext sets user, group, filesystem, capability and privilege options on a Pod or container.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-pod-security-c2","t":"Pod Security Standards","p":"Three built-in policy levels define what a Pod may do: privileged (anything), baseline (blocks known escalations) and restricted (current hardening best practice).","k":[["What are the three Pod Security Admission modes?","`enforce` rejects violations, `audit` records them in the audit log, `warn` shows a warning to the user."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-pod-security-c3","t":"Rolling out Pod Security safely","p":"Start with warn and audit, fix what they report, then enforce.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-pod-security-c4","t":"Capabilities, seccomp and AppArmor","p":"Linux splits root power into capabilities, filters syscalls with seccomp and confines files and network with AppArmor or SELinux.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-pod-security-c5","t":"Isolation beyond defaults","p":"When tenants are untrusted, add stronger boundaries: sandboxed runtimes, user namespaces or dedicated nodes.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s23-network-policy","title":"NetworkPolicy and segmentation","c":[{"id":"s23-network-policy-c1","t":"How NetworkPolicy works","p":"A NetworkPolicy selects Pods and lists which traffic is allowed to or from them. Once a Pod is selected by any policy for a direction, everything not allowed in that direction is denied.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s23-network-policy-c2","t":"Allowing application traffic","p":"Add small allow policies per tier: frontend to API, API to database, ingress controller to frontend.","k":[["In one `from` item you list namespaceSelector and podSelector together. AND or OR?","AND: the Pod must match both. Separate list items are OR."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s23-network-policy-c3","t":"Testing and observing policies","p":"Policies are easy to get wrong and fail silently. Test with real traffic and observe drops.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s23-network-policy-c4","t":"Limits of NetworkPolicy and extended policies","p":"The standard API is layer 3 and 4 only. Larger needs use CNI-specific or newer APIs.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s24-admission-policy","title":"Admission webhooks and policy engines","c":[{"id":"s24-admission-policy-c1","t":"Admission webhooks","p":"A webhook admission controller sends the request to an HTTPS service you run. The service answers allow, deny or a patch.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s24-admission-policy-c2","t":"ValidatingAdmissionPolicy with CEL","p":"ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (VAP) runs validation rules written in CEL inside the API server, with no webhook service. It is stable since 1.30.","k":[["Why use VAP before writing a webhook?","It runs inside the API server, needs no service or certificates and cannot be down."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s24-admission-policy-c3","t":"Kyverno","p":"Kyverno is a Kubernetes-native policy engine whose policies are plain YAML. It can validate, mutate, generate and verify images.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s24-admission-policy-c4","t":"OPA Gatekeeper","p":"Gatekeeper enforces policies written in Rego through ConstraintTemplates (the logic) and Constraints (where and how it applies).","k":[],"o":[["ValidatingAdmissionPolicy","simple field checks with no extra install","you need mutate, generate or cross-resource lookups"],["Kyverno","Kubernetes teams who want YAML policies and generation","you require a single language shared with non-Kubernetes OPA"],["Gatekeeper","complex logic and shared Rego across systems","your team does not want to learn Rego"]]},{"id":"s24-admission-policy-c5","t":"Operating policy safely","p":"Policies are production code. Roll them out in stages, test them and watch the webhooks.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s25-supply-chain-runtime","title":"Image policy, supply chain and runtime security","c":[{"id":"s25-supply-chain-runtime-c1","t":"Image provenance and registries","p":"Pull only from registries you control, reference images by digest, and keep the build chain trustworthy.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s25-supply-chain-runtime-c2","t":"Signing and verification","p":"Signing proves who built an image and that it has not changed. Admission verifies the signature before the Pod runs.","k":[["What does `mutateDigest: true` give you?","Admission rewrites the tag to the verified digest, so the Pod runs exactly what was verified."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s25-supply-chain-runtime-c3","t":"SBOMs and vulnerability scanning","p":"An SBOM lists every package in an image. Scanners compare that list against vulnerability databases.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s25-supply-chain-runtime-c4","t":"Runtime security","p":"Runtime tools watch what containers actually do and alert on unexpected behaviour: shells in containers, unexpected network calls, writes to sensitive files.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s25-supply-chain-runtime-c5","t":"Audit logs and detection","p":"Kubernetes audit logs record every API request: who, what, when and the result. They are the main record for investigations.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s25-supply-chain-runtime-c6","t":"A layered security checklist","p":"No single control is enough. Combine identity, admission, network and runtime layers and verify them regularly.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s26-helm-fundamentals","title":"Helm fundamentals","c":[{"id":"s26-helm-fundamentals-c1","t":"Charts, releases and repositories","p":"A chart is a folder of templated Kubernetes YAML plus default settings. A release is one installed copy of a chart in a cluster, with a name you choose. A repository is a place that serves charts, either a classic HTTP index or an OCI registry.","k":[["Does Helm need a controller running in the cluster?","No. Helm 3 and later is client-only and stores release state in the cluster as Secrets."],["What is the difference between a chart and a release?","A chart is the package; a release is one named installation of it with a specific values set."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s26-helm-fundamentals-c2","t":"Anatomy of a chart","p":"A chart is a directory with a fixed layout. Chart.yaml describes it, values.yaml holds defaults, and templates/ holds the YAML with placeholders.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s26-helm-fundamentals-c3","t":"Values and their precedence","p":"Values are the inputs to a chart. The chart ships defaults; you override them with files or command-line flags. Later sources win over earlier ones.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s26-helm-fundamentals-c4","t":"Templates and built-in objects","p":"Templates are YAML files with Go template expressions in double braces. Helm gives them objects such as .Values, .Release and .Chart to read.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s26-helm-fundamentals-c5","t":"Install, upgrade, rollback","p":"helm upgrade --install is the one idempotent command for CI: it installs if the release is missing and upgrades otherwise. Every change makes a new revision, and helm rollback returns to an earlier one.","k":[["What does --atomic do on failure?","It rolls the release back to the previous revision and returns a non-zero exit code."],["Why can a rollback of a Helm release not undo a database migration?","Helm only reapplies old manifests; it knows nothing about data changes made by the application."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s26-helm-fundamentals-c6","t":"Where release state lives and how to inspect it","p":"Helm stores each revision as a Secret in the release namespace, named sh.helm.release.v1.NAME.vN. It contains the chart, the values and the rendered manifest, compressed.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s27-helm-templating-dependencies","title":"Helm templating in depth and dependencies","c":[{"id":"s27-helm-templating-dependencies-c1","t":"Conditionals, with and range","p":"Templates can include a block only when a condition holds (if), loop over a list or map (range), and change the current scope (with).","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s27-helm-templating-dependencies-c2","t":"Named templates and _helpers.tpl","p":"A named template is a reusable snippet defined with define and called with include. The convention is to keep them in templates/helpers.tpl.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s27-helm-templating-dependencies-c3","t":"Validation with required, fail and schema","p":"You can make a chart refuse bad input early: required stops rendering when a value is missing, fail stops with your message, and values.schema.json validates the whole values tree.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s27-helm-templating-dependencies-c4","t":"Dependencies and subcharts","p":"A chart can depend on other charts, such as PostgreSQL or Redis. They are declared in Chart.yaml, downloaded into charts/, and configured through a section of your values named after the dependency.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s27-helm-templating-dependencies-c5","t":"Library charts and chart tests","p":"A library chart (type: library) contains only named templates and renders nothing by itself. Other charts depend on it to share conventions. Chart tests are Pods marked as tests that helm test runs against an installed release.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s27-helm-templating-dependencies-c6","t":"Debugging a template","p":"When rendering fails or produces odd YAML, you inspect the rendered output instead of guessing. helm template and --debug show what Helm would send.","k":[["Why use include instead of template for a labels helper?","include returns a string, so you can pipe it to nindent; template writes directly and cannot be piped."],["Where do subchart values go in the parent values file?","Under a key with the subchart name, for example postgresql, or under global to reach all charts."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s28-helm-hooks-oci-delivery","title":"Helm hooks, diff, OCI charts and delivery","c":[{"id":"s28-helm-hooks-oci-delivery-c1","t":"Hooks","p":"A hook is a manifest in a chart that Helm runs at a chosen moment of the release lifecycle, such as before an upgrade, instead of treating it as a normal part of the release.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s28-helm-hooks-oci-delivery-c2","t":"Hook delete policies and pitfalls","p":"Delete policies decide when Helm removes a hook object. Without one, a completed Job from the last run remains and the next run fails with \"already exists\".","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s28-helm-hooks-oci-delivery-c3","t":"helm diff and safe upgrades","p":"helm diff is a plugin that renders the new revision and shows what would change compared with the live release, in the same style as kubectl diff.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s28-helm-hooks-oci-delivery-c4","t":"OCI charts and registries","p":"Helm can store charts in any OCI registry (ECR, Harbor, GHCR, Artifact Registry), the same place you keep container images. A chart is addressed as oci://registry/path/name.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s28-helm-hooks-oci-delivery-c5","t":"Signing, provenance and post-renderers","p":"You can sign a chart so consumers verify it was not altered, and you can pipe Helm's rendered output through a program (a post-renderer) before it is applied.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s28-helm-hooks-oci-delivery-c6","t":"Helm in pipelines and GitOps","p":"Two common models exist. In pipeline driven delivery your CI runs helm upgrade. In GitOps a controller in the cluster (Argo CD or Flux) watches Git and applies the chart itself.","k":[["Why add before-hook-creation to a migration Job?","Otherwise the Job from the previous run still exists and the next hook fails with already exists."],["What does helm diff --detailed-exitcode return when changes exist?","Exit code 2."]],"o":[["Pipeline helm upgrade","a small number of clusters and a team new to GitOps","many clusters or a need for continuous drift correction"],["Flux HelmRelease","you want real Helm releases with GitOps pull","you need rich UI and app of apps views"],["Argo CD Application","you want a UI, multi cluster and sync waves","you rely on helm test or helm list showing the release"]]}]},{"id":"s29-kustomize-bases-overlays","title":"Kustomize bases and overlays","c":[{"id":"s29-kustomize-bases-overlays-c1","t":"What Kustomize is","p":"Kustomize reads a kustomization.yaml that lists resources and the changes to apply to them, then prints the final manifests. The original files are never modified.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s29-kustomize-bases-overlays-c2","t":"Bases and overlays","p":"A base holds the shared manifests. An overlay is another directory whose kustomization.yaml points at the base and adds environment-specific changes.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s29-kustomize-bases-overlays-c3","t":"The base manifests","p":"The base is ordinary, valid YAML that could be applied by itself. It uses no placeholders.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s29-kustomize-bases-overlays-c4","t":"Image, replica, namespace and name transformers","p":"Built-in transformers change common fields everywhere they appear: image tags, replica counts, namespace, name prefix and suffix, labels and annotations.","k":[["Why not change the image with sed?","sed matches text; Kustomize edits the right field in every workload structurally and keeps the YAML valid."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s29-kustomize-bases-overlays-c5","t":"ConfigMap and Secret generators","p":"A generator creates a ConfigMap or Secret from files or literals and adds a hash of the content to its name. When the content changes, the name changes, and every workload that references it is updated and rolled.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s29-kustomize-bases-overlays-c6","t":"Replacements and vars","p":"replacements copy a value from one field of one object into fields of other objects, for example a hostname defined once and used in an Ingress and a ConfigMap.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s30-kustomize-patches-components-helm","title":"Kustomize patches, components and Helm together","c":[{"id":"s30-kustomize-patches-components-helm-c1","t":"Strategic merge patches","p":"A strategic merge patch is a partial manifest. You write only the fields you want to change, with enough identity (kind and name) to find the target, and Kustomize merges it in.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s30-kustomize-patches-components-helm-c2","t":"JSON 6902 patches","p":"A JSON patch is an explicit list of operations (add, remove, replace) on paths in the document. It is precise and works on any object, including custom resources.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s30-kustomize-patches-components-helm-c3","t":"Components","p":"A component is a reusable bundle of resources and patches for an optional feature. Overlays opt in by listing it under components:.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s30-kustomize-patches-components-helm-c4","t":"Remote bases and the helmCharts generator","p":"A kustomization can use a Git repository as a base, and can inflate a Helm chart into plain YAML so you can patch it.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s30-kustomize-patches-components-helm-c5","t":"Helm or Kustomize","p":"They solve different problems. Helm packages and distributes software with logic and a release lifecycle. Kustomize adapts YAML you own for several environments without logic.","k":[],"o":[["Helm","you install vendor software or ship your app to many users","the only difference is replicas and image tag per environment"],["Kustomize","you own the YAML and need per environment overlays","you need hooks, conditionals or a distributable package"],["Helm plus Kustomize","you must patch a chart you cannot change","a plain values override already does the job"]]},{"id":"s30-kustomize-patches-components-helm-c6","t":"Validating rendered output","p":"Whatever generates your YAML, the final result should be checked before it reaches the cluster: schema validity, policy rules and live diff.","k":[["How do overlays opt in to optional features?","By listing components in the components field of their kustomization."],["Which patch style works well on a custom resource?","A JSON 6902 patch, because it needs no schema knowledge."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s31-observability-signals","title":"Observability signals in Kubernetes","c":[{"id":"s31-observability-signals-c1","t":"Status, conditions and describe","p":"Every object has a spec (what you asked for) and a status (what the controller observed). Conditions are named true or false facts inside status, such as Ready or PodScheduled, each with a reason and message.","k":[["Where do you find why the previous container instance died?","In status.containerStatuses[].lastState.terminated, which shows reason, exit code and timestamps."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s31-observability-signals-c2","t":"Events","p":"Events are short records created by the scheduler, kubelet and controllers: \"Scheduled\", \"Pulling image\", \"FailedMount\", \"BackOff\". They explain what happened to an object.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s31-observability-signals-c3","t":"metrics-server and kubectl top","p":"metrics-server is a small add-on that collects current CPU and memory use from each kubelet and exposes it through the Metrics API. kubectl top and the HorizontalPodAutoscaler read it.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s31-observability-signals-c4","t":"Container logs","p":"Containers write to standard output and standard error. The container runtime stores those streams as files on the node, and kubectl logs reads them through the kubelet.","k":[["Why does kubectl logs --previous matter for CrashLoopBackOff?","The current container may have just restarted and be empty; the useful error is in the previous instance."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s31-observability-signals-c5","t":"Cluster-level logging architecture","p":"Kubernetes itself does not store logs long term. A log agent runs on every node as a DaemonSet, reads the container log files, adds Pod labels and sends them to a backend.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s31-observability-signals-c6","t":"Metrics and the Prometheus model","p":"Prometheus pulls numeric time series from HTTP endpoints. In Kubernetes it discovers targets through the API, so new Pods are scraped automatically.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s31-observability-signals-c7","t":"Probes, API health and control plane signals","p":"Readiness, liveness and startup probes tell the kubelet about application health. Separately, the API server exposes /readyz and /livez, and control plane components expose metrics.","k":[["A Pod is Running but receives no traffic. Which probe is likely failing?","The readiness probe; the Pod is removed from the Service endpoints."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s32-troubleshooting-pods","title":"Troubleshooting playbook, Pod startup and runtime","c":[{"id":"s32-troubleshooting-pods-c1","t":"The troubleshooting method","p":"Work from the outside in and from the object to its owner: is it scheduled, is it started, is it ready, is it reachable. Each stage has its own evidence.","k":[["Why start with events rather than logs for a Pending Pod?","A Pending Pod has not started, so it has no logs. The scheduler explains itself in events."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s32-troubleshooting-pods-c2","t":"Pending Pods","p":"A Pod stays Pending when no node can run it yet. The scheduler tells you the reason in a FailedScheduling event.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s32-troubleshooting-pods-c3","t":"ImagePullBackOff and ErrImagePull","p":"The kubelet could not download the container image. It retries with an increasing delay, which is the BackOff in the name.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s32-troubleshooting-pods-c4","t":"CrashLoopBackOff","p":"The container starts, exits, and the kubelet restarts it with a delay that doubles from 10 seconds up to 5 minutes. CrashLoopBackOff is a symptom, never a cause.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s32-troubleshooting-pods-c5","t":"OOMKilled and memory limits","p":"When a container uses more memory than its limit, the kernel's OOM killer ends it. Kubernetes records Reason: OOMKilled with exit code 137.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s32-troubleshooting-pods-c6","t":"Evictions and node pressure","p":"When a node runs low on memory, disk or process IDs, the kubelet evicts Pods to protect the node. Evicted Pods show status Failed with reason Evicted.","k":[["Which resource is incompressible, CPU or memory?","Memory. CPU is throttled; memory over the limit causes an OOM kill."],["Why do Guaranteed Pods survive evictions longest?","Their requests equal their limits, so they never use more than they asked for, which is the first eviction criterion."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s33-troubleshooting-network-node-storage","title":"Troubleshooting playbook, network, nodes and storage","c":[{"id":"s33-troubleshooting-network-node-storage-c1","t":"Service has no endpoints","p":"A Service forwards to the Pods that match its selector and are Ready. If the endpoint list is empty, connections fail even though Pods are running.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s33-troubleshooting-network-node-storage-c2","t":"DNS failures","p":"Pods resolve names through CoreDNS. Names look like service.namespace.svc.cluster.local, and short names work through search domains in the Pod's /etc/resolv.conf.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s33-troubleshooting-network-node-storage-c3","t":"NetworkPolicy blocking traffic","p":"If a Pod is selected by a NetworkPolicy, only traffic that a policy allows can reach it (ingress) or leave it (egress). A missing allow rule looks exactly like a network fault: timeouts, not refusals.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s33-troubleshooting-network-node-storage-c4","t":"Nodes in NotReady","p":"A node is NotReady when the kubelet stops reporting healthy to the control plane. After a grace period the node gets taints and its Pods are evicted.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s33-troubleshooting-network-node-storage-c5","t":"PVC stuck Pending or Terminating","p":"A PersistentVolumeClaim is Pending until a volume is bound to it. A claim or Pod stuck in Terminating usually waits on a finalizer, a mount or a detach.","k":[["Why does a PVC stay Pending with WaitForFirstConsumer?","Binding is delayed until a Pod using it is scheduled, so the volume is created in the right zone. It is normal until a consumer exists."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s33-troubleshooting-network-node-storage-c6","t":"kubectl debug and ephemeral containers","p":"kubectl debug adds a temporary container with your tools to a running Pod, or makes a modified copy of a Pod, or opens a shell on a node.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s34-upgrade-strategy-skew-apis","title":"Upgrade strategy, version skew and deprecated APIs","c":[{"id":"s34-upgrade-strategy-skew-apis-c1","t":"Release cadence and support windows","p":"Kubernetes releases a new minor version (1.33, 1.34, 1.35 and so on) about three times a year. The community supports the newest three minor versions with patches. Managed services add their own windows.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s34-upgrade-strategy-skew-apis-c2","t":"Version skew rules","p":"Components of a cluster may run different minor versions, but only within strict limits. You upgrade the control plane first, then nodes, then clients.","k":[["Can a kubelet be newer than the API server?","No. The kubelet must not be newer than the kube-apiserver."],["Can you jump from 1.31 straight to 1.34 on the control plane?","No. Control plane upgrades go one minor version at a time."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s34-upgrade-strategy-skew-apis-c3","t":"Deprecated and removed APIs","p":"Kubernetes deprecates API versions (for example policy/v1beta1 PodDisruptionBudget) and later removes them. Manifests using a removed version stop applying, and controllers that still call it fail.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s34-upgrade-strategy-skew-apis-c4","t":"Scanning with pluto and kubent","p":"pluto and kube-no-trouble (kubent) scan manifests, Helm releases or a live cluster and report API versions that are deprecated or removed in a given Kubernetes version.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s34-upgrade-strategy-skew-apis-c5","t":"Pre-upgrade checklist","p":"A short, written checklist turns an upgrade from a risky event into a repeatable procedure.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s34-upgrade-strategy-skew-apis-c6","t":"Upgrading the control plane","p":"The control plane is upgraded as one operation by the provider. You start it, wait, and verify. For self-managed clusters, kubeadm upgrade does the same steps on each control plane node.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s35-node-upgrades-drain-pdb","title":"Node upgrades, drain and PodDisruptionBudgets","c":[{"id":"s35-node-upgrades-drain-pdb-c1","t":"Cordon, drain and the eviction API","p":"Cordon marks a node unschedulable. Drain cordons and then asks every Pod on the node to leave using the Eviction API, which respects PodDisruptionBudgets and graceful shutdown.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s35-node-upgrades-drain-pdb-c2","t":"PodDisruptionBudgets","p":"A PodDisruptionBudget (PDB) limits how many Pods of a set can be down because of voluntary disruptions such as drain. It does not stop crashes or node failures.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s35-node-upgrades-drain-pdb-c3","t":"Why a drain hangs","p":"A drain finishes only when every evictable Pod has left. A single blocker keeps it waiting forever unless you set a timeout.","k":[["What does a PDB protect against?","Voluntary disruptions through the Eviction API, such as drain and autoscaler scale down. It does not prevent crashes or node failure."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s35-node-upgrades-drain-pdb-c4","t":"Graceful shutdown for Pods","p":"When a Pod is deleted, it is removed from Service endpoints and the container receives SIGTERM at roughly the same time. A preStop hook and terminationGracePeriodSeconds give the app time to finish.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s35-node-upgrades-drain-pdb-c5","t":"Node upgrade strategies","p":"Nodes are upgraded either in place (update software on the same machine), by rolling replacement (new nodes join, old ones drain and leave), or by blue-green node pools.","k":[],"o":[["Surge rolling replacement","the default for managed node groups and Gardener pools","zero spare capacity or quota for extra nodes"],["Blue-green pool","big version jumps, new OS or instance family, easy rollback","IP space or quota is tight"],["In place","small self-managed clusters you can rebuild","production where a failed node upgrade must be reversible"]]},{"id":"s35-node-upgrades-drain-pdb-c6","t":"Node rotation and Gardener notes","p":"Nodes should be rotated regularly, not only on Kubernetes upgrades. Gardener (the system under Kyma and SAP's managed Kubernetes) does this through maintenance windows and worker pool settings.","k":[["Why spread replicas across zones and nodes?","So a drain or node loss removes only some replicas and the PDB can still allow evictions."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s35-node-upgrades-drain-pdb-c7","t":"After the upgrade, verify and roll back","p":"After each step you verify cluster health with a short, fixed set of commands. Rolling back means undoing what you can: node pools, add-ons and manifests. The control plane version cannot be downgraded.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s36-cluster-lifecycle-eks-gardener","title":"Cluster lifecycle on EKS and Gardener","c":[{"id":"s36-cluster-lifecycle-eks-gardener-c1","t":"Clusters as code","p":"Create clusters from declarative definitions (Terraform, eksctl, Gardener Shoot manifests, Crossplane) stored in Git, not by clicking in a console.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s36-cluster-lifecycle-eks-gardener-c2","t":"EKS managed add-ons","p":"EKS can install and upgrade core components (VPC CNI, CoreDNS, kube-proxy, EBS CSI driver, Pod Identity agent) as managed add-ons with versions that are tested against each Kubernetes version.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s36-cluster-lifecycle-eks-gardener-c3","t":"Gardener shoots and Kyma runtimes","p":"Gardener manages Kubernetes clusters called shoots. A shoot's control plane runs as Pods in another cluster (the seed), and workers run in your infrastructure account. The Kyma runtime on SAP BTP is a Gardener shoot with Kyma modules enabled.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s36-cluster-lifecycle-eks-gardener-c4","t":"Add-on inventory and GitOps for platform components","p":"Keep one list of everything that runs in kube-system and platform namespaces, with version and owner, and install it from Git with a GitOps controller.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s36-cluster-lifecycle-eks-gardener-c5","t":"Backup and disaster recovery","p":"The provider protects the control plane and etcd. You protect your application data, your manifests and your ability to rebuild.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s36-cluster-lifecycle-eks-gardener-c6","t":"Replace rather than repair","p":"For major changes, build a new cluster beside the old one and move traffic, instead of mutating the old one in place.","k":[["Who backs up your application data on EKS?","You do. AWS operates the control plane and etcd, but volumes and databases need your own snapshots or backups."]],"o":[["In-place upgrade","routine minor upgrades with good test coverage","you also change CNI mode, IP family or other one-way settings"],["Blue-green cluster","large changes, risky add-on swaps, compliance rebuilds","you cannot move stateful data or afford double capacity"]]}]},{"id":"s37-service-mesh-concepts","title":"Service mesh concepts and data planes","c":[{"id":"s37-service-mesh-concepts-c1","t":"What a service mesh does","p":"A mesh adds a managed layer between your services. A data plane handles the traffic; a control plane configures it. Applications keep making ordinary HTTP or gRPC calls.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s37-service-mesh-concepts-c2","t":"The sidecar model","p":"In the classic design, a proxy container (Envoy for Istio, linkerd2-proxy for Linkerd) is injected into every Pod. Traffic in and out of the application container passes through it.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s37-service-mesh-concepts-c3","t":"The ambient model","p":"Istio ambient mode removes per-Pod sidecars. A small agent on each node (ztunnel) handles L4 features such as mTLS and identity. Optional waypoint proxies per namespace or service handle L7 features like HTTP routing.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s37-service-mesh-concepts-c4","t":"Istio, Linkerd and Cilium compared","p":"Three popular meshes make different trade-offs. Istio has the broadest features, Linkerd aims for simplicity and a small footprint, and Cilium builds networking, policy and optional mesh features on eBPF.","k":[],"o":[["Istio","you need rich L7 traffic control, a Kyma/SAP integrated mesh or multi cluster","the team cannot invest in operating it"],["Linkerd","you want mTLS and golden metrics with the least complexity","you need advanced routing and extension points"],["Cilium","you already use it as CNI and want policy plus flow visibility","you need the broad L7 feature set of Envoy meshes"],["No mesh","few services, ingress TLS and NetworkPolicy are enough","compliance requires mTLS everywhere"]]},{"id":"s37-service-mesh-concepts-c5","t":"Costs and failure modes","p":"A mesh adds a new moving part on the path of every request. Know the overhead and the typical failure modes before you enable it.","k":[["Does an Istio control plane outage stop traffic between running Pods?","No. Proxies keep the last configuration they received. New or changed config and new Pods are affected."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s37-service-mesh-concepts-c6","t":"Adopting a mesh step by step","p":"Introduce a mesh gradually: observe first, then secure, then add traffic features, one namespace at a time.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s38-istio-traffic-security","title":"Istio traffic management and security","c":[{"id":"s38-istio-traffic-security-c1","t":"Gateways for ingress","p":"A gateway is an Envoy proxy at the edge of the mesh that accepts external traffic. You configure it with the Kubernetes Gateway API (Gateway, HTTPRoute) or Istio's own Gateway and VirtualService.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s38-istio-traffic-security-c2","t":"Weighted routing and canaries","p":"Traffic can be split between versions by percentage, independent of the number of Pods. This allows a canary release that sends 5 percent of requests to the new version.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s38-istio-traffic-security-c3","t":"Retries, timeouts and circuit breaking","p":"The mesh can enforce time limits, retry failed calls, and eject unhealthy backends, so these policies are consistent for all services.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s38-istio-traffic-security-c4","t":"mTLS and PeerAuthentication","p":"Istio gives every workload a certificate based identity (a SPIFFE ID built from namespace and service account) and encrypts traffic between proxies. PeerAuthentication controls whether plain traffic is still accepted.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s38-istio-traffic-security-c5","t":"AuthorizationPolicy","p":"An AuthorizationPolicy allows or denies requests based on who is calling (identity or namespace), where it goes (host, method, path) and request attributes such as headers or JWT claims.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s38-istio-traffic-security-c6","t":"Debugging Istio","p":"Most mesh problems are configuration mismatches or startup ordering. Istio ships tools that compare what you wrote with what each proxy actually received.","k":[["What does an empty AuthorizationPolicy do?","It selects all workloads in the namespace with no allow rules, so it denies all requests."],["Which Istio resource limits connections and ejects failing hosts?","DestinationRule, through connectionPool and outlierDetection."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s39-linkerd-cilium-mesh-ops","title":"Linkerd, Cilium and operating a mesh","c":[{"id":"s39-linkerd-cilium-mesh-ops-c1","t":"Linkerd in practice","p":"Linkerd injects a lightweight Rust proxy into each Pod and enables mTLS and golden metrics (success rate, request rate, latency) with no extra configuration.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s39-linkerd-cilium-mesh-ops-c2","t":"Cilium as CNI and mesh","p":"Cilium uses eBPF programs in the Linux kernel to route packets, enforce NetworkPolicy and observe traffic. It can replace kube-proxy and offers mesh features without a sidecar per Pod.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s39-linkerd-cilium-mesh-ops-c3","t":"Seeing traffic with Hubble","p":"Hubble records network flows with Kubernetes identity (namespace, Pod, labels) and the policy verdict for each, so you can answer \"why is this connection dropped\" directly.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s39-linkerd-cilium-mesh-ops-c4","t":"Observing the mesh with metrics and traces","p":"Proxies emit uniform metrics for every service pair and propagate trace headers. Prometheus scrapes the metrics; a tracing backend receives spans.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s39-linkerd-cilium-mesh-ops-c5","t":"Upgrading and troubleshooting a mesh","p":"Treat the mesh as a platform component with its own upgrade process: control plane first, then data plane, in a canary fashion.","k":[["Why must apps forward trace headers when a mesh is present?","The proxy creates spans per hop but cannot know which inbound request caused an outbound call unless the application passes the trace context along."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s40-namespaces-quotas-limitranges","title":"Namespaces, quotas and LimitRange","c":[{"id":"s40-namespaces-quotas-limitranges-c1","t":"Tenancy models","p":"There is a range from \"everyone in one cluster\" to \"one cluster per tenant\". Namespaces give soft isolation inside a cluster. Separate clusters give hard isolation.","k":[],"o":[["Namespace per team","internal teams who share a trust boundary","tenants that must not share a kernel or API server"],["Virtual cluster","teams need their own CRDs, cluster admin and CI environments","strict kernel level isolation is required"],["Cluster per tenant","regulation, noisy or hostile tenants, different versions","you cannot afford the operations overhead"]]},{"id":"s40-namespaces-quotas-limitranges-c2","t":"Namespaces","p":"A namespace is a named scope inside a cluster. Most objects (Pods, Services, Secrets, ConfigMaps, Roles) live in exactly one namespace, and names must be unique only within it.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s40-namespaces-quotas-limitranges-c3","t":"ResourceQuota","p":"A ResourceQuota caps the total resources a namespace may consume or create: CPU and memory requests and limits, storage, and counts of objects.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s40-namespaces-quotas-limitranges-c4","t":"LimitRange","p":"A LimitRange sets default requests and limits for containers that do not specify any, and minimum and maximum values per container, Pod or PersistentVolumeClaim.","k":[["Why do Pods get rejected after you add a compute ResourceQuota?","Quota requires every Pod to declare requests and limits for the quota'd resources. Add them or provide a LimitRange with defaults."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s40-namespaces-quotas-limitranges-c5","t":"Priority classes and fairness","p":"When capacity is short, PriorityClass decides which Pods may preempt others. API Priority and Fairness protects the API server from one noisy client.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s40-namespaces-quotas-limitranges-c6","t":"A namespace template for onboarding","p":"Create tenant namespaces from one template so every team gets the same guardrails: labels, quota, LimitRange, default-deny NetworkPolicy, Pod Security level and RBAC.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s41-hierarchical-vcluster-isolation","title":"Hierarchical tenancy, vcluster and node isolation","c":[{"id":"s41-hierarchical-vcluster-isolation-c1","t":"Hierarchical namespaces and Capsule","p":"Organisations have teams that contain sub-teams. Tools such as the Hierarchical Namespace Controller (HNC) and Capsule let you group namespaces under a tenant and propagate policy from parent to children.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s41-hierarchical-vcluster-isolation-c2","t":"Virtual clusters with vcluster","p":"vcluster runs a complete Kubernetes API server (and its own data store) as Pods inside a namespace of a host cluster. The tenant gets a kubeconfig for the virtual cluster, where they are cluster admin.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s41-hierarchical-vcluster-isolation-c3","t":"Dedicated nodes with taints and affinity","p":"Reserve nodes for a tenant or workload class by tainting them and adding a matching toleration and node selector to that tenant's Pods.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s41-hierarchical-vcluster-isolation-c4","t":"Sandboxed runtimes and stronger isolation","p":"Containers share the host kernel. For untrusted code, a RuntimeClass can select a sandbox (gVisor, Kata Containers, Firecracker based) that puts a smaller kernel or a micro VM between the workload and the host.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s41-hierarchical-vcluster-isolation-c5","t":"Fair sharing with Kueue and noisy neighbours","p":"Beyond quotas, batch-heavy clusters use a queueing system such as Kueue to admit jobs by tenant share, and Pod-level controls to limit noisy neighbours.","k":[["Do taints alone make a node pool exclusive?","No. A taint repels other Pods, but the tenant's Pods also need a node selector or affinity so they do not land on shared nodes."],["Does vcluster isolate the kernel?","No. It isolates the API. Pods still share the host kernel unless you add dedicated nodes or a sandboxed runtime."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s42-cost-allocation-finops","title":"Cost allocation and FinOps on Kubernetes","c":[{"id":"s42-cost-allocation-finops-c1","t":"Why cost is hard to attribute","p":"You pay for nodes, disks, load balancers and network traffic. Pods share those. Allocation rules decide how a node's price is split among the Pods that ran on it.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s42-cost-allocation-finops-c2","t":"Labels and tagging for cost","p":"Consistent labels on namespaces and workloads (and tags on cloud resources) are what let tools attribute cost to a team.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s42-cost-allocation-finops-c3","t":"OpenCost and Kubecost","p":"OpenCost is the open source CNCF project that calculates Kubernetes cost from Prometheus metrics and cloud price lists. Kubecost is a commercial product built on the same engine, adding history, alerts and multi-cluster views.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s42-cost-allocation-finops-c4","t":"Showback and chargeback","p":"Showback reports cost to teams for awareness. Chargeback actually bills their budget. Start with showback.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s42-cost-allocation-finops-c5","t":"Reducing cost safely","p":"After you can see cost, reduce it in order of safety: remove waste, right-size, then change purchasing and scheduling.","k":[["Why can a Pod with low CPU usage still cost a lot?","Cost is based on reserved requests, so an oversized request holds node capacity whether or not the Pod uses it."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s43-crds-custom-resources","title":"CRDs and custom resources","c":[{"id":"s43-crds-custom-resources-c1","t":"What a CRD is","p":"A CustomResourceDefinition (CRD) teaches the API server a new object type. After you apply it, you can create, read and watch objects of that kind. A CRD stores data only; behaviour comes from a controller.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s43-crds-custom-resources-c2","t":"Writing a CRD","p":"A CRD names the type, lists versions, and gives an OpenAPI v3 schema describing allowed fields.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s43-crds-custom-resources-c3","t":"Custom resources in use","p":"Once the CRD exists, you create objects of the new kind like any other manifest and manage them with the usual commands.","k":[["What happens to existing custom resources if you delete their CRD?","They are all deleted, because the API no longer knows the type."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s43-crds-custom-resources-c4","t":"Validation rules with CEL","p":"Beyond schema types, CRDs can carry CEL (Common Expression Language) rules that check relations between fields, evaluated by the API server.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s43-crds-custom-resources-c5","t":"Versions and conversion","p":"A CRD can serve several versions (v1alpha1, v1beta1, v1) of the same type. The API converts between them, while only one version is stored in etcd.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s43-crds-custom-resources-c6","t":"Installing and upgrading CRDs safely","p":"CRDs are cluster-wide and shared by everyone. Their lifecycle needs more care than ordinary workload manifests.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s44-controllers-operator-pattern","title":"Controllers and the operator pattern","c":[{"id":"s44-controllers-operator-pattern-c1","t":"The reconcile loop","p":"A controller repeatedly compares desired state (spec) with observed state (the real world) and takes actions to reduce the difference. It does not react to events as commands; it re-evaluates the whole situation.","k":[["Why should a reconciler not assume it sees every event?","Controllers are level triggered: events can be coalesced, dropped or replayed after restart. The reconciler must derive its actions from current state."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s44-controllers-operator-pattern-c2","t":"Informers, caches and the work queue","p":"Controllers do not poll the API server. An informer opens a watch, keeps a local cache of objects, and notifies handlers when something changes.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s44-controllers-operator-pattern-c3","t":"The operator pattern","p":"An operator is a CRD plus a controller that automates the operational tasks a skilled human would do for a particular application: install, configure, back up, upgrade, fail over.","k":[],"o":[["Operator","stateful or complex software with ordered lifecycle steps","a stateless app a Deployment and Helm chart can run"],["Helm chart only","static installs and upgrades with no runtime logic","day-2 tasks such as failover must be automated"],["Managed service","an RDS, Cloud SQL or HANA Cloud instance meets the need","you must stay in the cluster or avoid vendor lock-in"],["Custom operator","no operator exists and the logic is truly specific to you","an existing, well maintained operator already does it"]]},{"id":"s44-controllers-operator-pattern-c4","t":"Anatomy of a controller-runtime reconciler","p":"controller-runtime is the Go library behind Kubebuilder and Operator SDK. You write one Reconcile function that receives a name and namespace and returns whether to requeue.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s44-controllers-operator-pattern-c5","t":"Status, conditions and generations","p":"A controller reports progress in status, using standard conditions, and records which version of the spec it has processed in observedGeneration.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s44-controllers-operator-pattern-c6","t":"Building operators and RBAC","p":"Frameworks scaffold the project: Kubebuilder and Operator SDK (Go), Kopf (Python), and Java Operator SDK. Whatever the language, the operator runs as a Deployment with a ServiceAccount and RBAC.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s45-finalizers-owners-garbage-collection","title":"Owner references, finalizers and garbage collection","c":[{"id":"s45-finalizers-owners-garbage-collection-c1","t":"Owner references","p":"An object can list its owner in metadata.ownerReferences. When the owner is deleted, the garbage collector deletes the dependants.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s45-finalizers-owners-garbage-collection-c2","t":"Garbage collection and deletion propagation","p":"When you delete an object you choose what happens to its dependants: delete them in the background, wait for them first (foreground), or leave them behind (orphan).","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s45-finalizers-owners-garbage-collection-c3","t":"Finalizers","p":"A finalizer is a string in metadata.finalizers that tells the API server \"do not remove this object yet\". Deletion sets deletionTimestamp, and the object stays until every finalizer has been removed by its controller.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s45-finalizers-owners-garbage-collection-c4","t":"Objects stuck in Terminating","p":"An object with a deletionTimestamp that never disappears is waiting for a finalizer. Find out which one before you remove it.","k":[["What does the API server do when you delete an object that has a finalizer?","It sets deletionTimestamp and keeps the object until every finalizer is removed by its controller."],["Name a safer first step than removing a finalizer.","Find the controller that owns the finalizer and restore it (or its CRD or dependency) so it can finish cleanup."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s45-finalizers-owners-garbage-collection-c5","t":"Server-side apply and field ownership","p":"With server-side apply (SSA), the API server tracks which manager owns each field (metadata.managedFields) and reports conflicts when two managers change the same field.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s46-admission-conversion-webhooks","title":"Admission and conversion webhooks","c":[{"id":"s46-admission-conversion-webhooks-c1","t":"The admission chain","p":"After a request is authenticated and authorised, it passes through admission. Mutating admission can change the object; validating admission can only accept or reject. Then the object is stored.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s46-admission-conversion-webhooks-c2","t":"Writing a validating webhook configuration","p":"A webhook configuration tells the API server which requests to send to which HTTPS endpoint, and what to do if it cannot be reached.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s46-admission-conversion-webhooks-c3","t":"The AdmissionReview exchange","p":"The API server POSTs an AdmissionReview JSON document containing the object and request details. The webhook answers with an AdmissionReview response with allowed true or false, and for mutation a JSON Patch.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s46-admission-conversion-webhooks-c4","t":"ValidatingAdmissionPolicy with CEL","p":"ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (VAP) lets you write validation rules in CEL that run inside the API server, with no webhook service to host. A binding says where the policy applies.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s46-admission-conversion-webhooks-c5","t":"Conversion webhooks","p":"A conversion webhook converts a custom resource between versions when a client asks for a version that differs from the stored one. It is the CRD counterpart of admission webhooks.","k":[["What happens to reads of a custom resource if its conversion webhook is down?","Requests that need conversion fail, because the API server cannot translate between the stored and requested versions."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s46-admission-conversion-webhooks-c6","t":"Debugging and hardening webhooks","p":"Webhook failures show up as odd API errors: timeouts when creating Pods, failed calling webhook, or Helm installs that hang. Know the symptoms and the emergency exit.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s47-api-in-depth","title":"The Kubernetes API in depth","c":[{"id":"s47-api-in-depth-c1","t":"API groups, versions and resources","p":"Resources are organised into groups such as apps, batch and networking.k8s.io. Each group has one or more versions such as v1. The old core group has no name and is written v1.","k":[["What is the group of Pods and Services?","The core group, which has no name. Its apiVersion is just v1 and its path starts with /api/v1."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s47-api-in-depth-c2","t":"Versions, stability levels and deprecation","p":"A version suffix tells you how stable an API is. v1 is stable, v1beta1 is a beta that may change, and v1alpha1 is experimental and off by default.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s47-api-in-depth-c3","t":"Discovery and OpenAPI","p":"The API server publishes what it serves. Clients read this at runtime so that kubectl can work with CRDs it has never seen.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s47-api-in-depth-c4","t":"Aggregated APIs and APIService","p":"An aggregated API lets another server join the main API under a group such as metrics.k8s.io. The kube-apiserver proxies matching requests to it.","k":[["Why can a broken metrics-server block namespace deletion?","The namespace controller discovers all APIs. An unavailable aggregated API makes discovery fail, so it cannot confirm that the namespace is empty."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s47-api-in-depth-c5","t":"Server-side apply","p":"With server-side apply (SSA) you send your desired fields and the API server merges them into the live object and records who owns each field.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s47-api-in-depth-c6","t":"Field managers and managedFields","p":"Each writer has a manager name. managedFields lists which manager set which fields, with the operation (Apply or Update) and a timestamp.","k":[["What does a 409 conflict on apply tell you?","Another field manager owns a field you tried to set to a different value. Decide who should own it, then drop the field or force."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s47-api-in-depth-c7","t":"Watch, resourceVersion and informers","p":"A watch is a long-lived HTTP stream of changes. Every object carries a resourceVersion that marks its place in etcd history, so a client can resume from a known point.","k":[["What should a client do after 410 Gone?","Perform a new LIST to obtain a fresh resourceVersion and then start a new watch."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s47-api-in-depth-c8","t":"API priority and fairness","p":"API Priority and Fairness (APF) is the API server's queueing system. It sorts requests into priority levels and flows so that one noisy client cannot starve the rest.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s48-kubectl-mastery","title":"kubectl mastery","c":[{"id":"s48-kubectl-mastery-c1","t":"Output formats and server-side printing","p":"-o changes how kubectl prints an object. Besides yaml and json, there are wide, name, jsonpath, custom-columns and go-template.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s48-kubectl-mastery-c2","t":"JSONPath","p":"JSONPath is a small query language over the object JSON. kubectl wraps it in braces: {.metadata.name}.","k":[["How do you read a Secret key named tls.crt in jsonpath?","Escape the dot, {.data.tls\\.crt}, and base64-decode the result."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s48-kubectl-mastery-c3","t":"Custom columns and templates","p":"-o custom-columns=HEADER:path,... makes a tidy table from any fields. go-template gives loops and conditions when jsonpath is not enough.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s48-kubectl-mastery-c4","t":"kubectl debug and ephemeral containers","p":"kubectl debug adds a temporary container with debugging tools to a running Pod, or copies a Pod, or starts a privileged Pod on a node.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s48-kubectl-mastery-c5","t":"Contexts, kubeconfig and safe switching","p":"A kubeconfig holds clusters, users and contexts. A context is a named triple of cluster, user and default namespace.","k":[["Why pass --context in scripts?","The current-context can change between runs or terminals, and an explicit context prevents running against the wrong cluster."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s48-kubectl-mastery-c6","t":"kubectl plugins","p":"Any executable on your PATH named kubectl-NAME is a plugin and runs as kubectl NAME.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s48-kubectl-mastery-c7","t":"krew and useful plugins","p":"krew is the plugin manager for kubectl. It installs community plugins from an index with kubectl krew install.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s48-kubectl-mastery-c8","t":"Apply, diff, patch and wait","p":"Beyond apply, kubectl has verbs to preview, change a single field and wait for conditions, which make scripts and pipelines reliable.","k":[["What does kubectl diff return when there are changes?","Exit code 1, so scripts can tell that a difference exists. Exit code above 1 means an error."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s49-gateway-api-deep","title":"Gateway API in depth","c":[{"id":"s49-gateway-api-deep-c1","t":"The role-oriented resource model","p":"Gateway API splits what Ingress mixed together. Infrastructure providers define a GatewayClass, cluster operators create Gateways, and app teams attach routes.","k":[["Which object does the application team normally own?","The route (HTTPRoute and similar). The Gateway belongs to the cluster operator, and the GatewayClass to the infrastructure provider."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s49-gateway-api-deep-c2","t":"Listeners, hostnames and allowedRoutes","p":"A listener is a port and protocol on a Gateway, optionally limited to a hostname. allowedRoutes decides which namespaces may attach routes to it.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s49-gateway-api-deep-c3","t":"HTTPRoute matching and filters","p":"Each rule has matches (path, headers, query, method) and optional filters that change the request or response before it reaches the backend.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s49-gateway-api-deep-c4","t":"Traffic splitting and canary","p":"backendRefs accept a weight. Traffic is divided in proportion to the weights, so canary and blue-green releases need no extra tool.","k":[["Are weights percentages?","No, they are relative shares. The share of a backend is its weight divided by the sum of weights."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s49-gateway-api-deep-c5","t":"TLS, certificates and cross-namespace references","p":"A Gateway listener terminates TLS using a Secret. If the Secret or a backend is in another namespace, a ReferenceGrant in the target namespace must allow it.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s49-gateway-api-deep-c6","t":"Policy attachment and implementations","p":"Policy attachment adds behaviour such as rate limits, retries or auth to a Gateway or Route by pointing a separate policy object at it using targetRefs.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s49-gateway-api-deep-c7","t":"Migrating from Ingress","p":"Migration means translating Ingress rules and annotations into Gateways and routes, then moving traffic gradually.","k":[["How can you test a new Gateway before changing DNS?","Call its address directly with curl --resolve host:port:IP so that the Host header and SNI still match."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s50-cni-deep-dive","title":"CNI deep dive, Cilium, Calico and eBPF","c":[{"id":"s50-cni-deep-dive-c1","t":"What a CNI plugin does","p":"The Container Network Interface is a small contract. When the runtime creates a Pod sandbox it calls the plugin with ADD, and the plugin creates the network interface and assigns an IP.","k":[["Why is a new node NotReady when the CNI is missing?","The kubelet reports the network plugin as not ready until a CNI config exists, so no Pods can get an interface."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s50-cni-deep-dive-c2","t":"Overlay, routed and native networking","p":"Pod traffic between nodes either travels inside a tunnel (overlay), is routed using the underlying network, or uses cloud-native addresses.","k":[],"o":[["Overlay","mixed networks and quick start","you can route pod CIDRs and want lowest overhead"],["BGP routed","on-premises with BGP capable switches","the network team cannot peer with nodes"],["VPC native (EKS)","you need security groups per Pod and VPC visibility","subnets are small and IPs scarce"]]},{"id":"s50-cni-deep-dive-c3","t":"Calico","p":"Calico is a mature CNI known for routing with BGP and a rich policy engine. It can use iptables, nftables or an eBPF dataplane.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s50-cni-deep-dive-c4","t":"Cilium and eBPF","p":"Cilium uses eBPF, small verified programs that run inside the Linux kernel, to route, filter and observe traffic without long iptables chains.","k":[["What does identity based policy avoid?","Dependence on Pod IPs, which change constantly. Policy follows label-derived identities."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s50-cni-deep-dive-c5","t":"Replacing kube-proxy","p":"kube-proxy programs iptables or IPVS rules to turn Service addresses into Pod addresses. Cilium and Calico eBPF can do this in the kernel instead.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s50-cni-deep-dive-c6","t":"Observability with Hubble","p":"Hubble shows network flows between Pods, including which policy allowed or dropped them, using data from the eBPF dataplane.","k":[["Which verdict shows a NetworkPolicy block?","DROPPED, along with the policy denial reason in the flow."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s50-cni-deep-dive-c7","t":"Choosing and migrating a CNI","p":"A CNI is a foundation decision. Changing it later usually means rebuilding nodes or clusters, so decide early and test upgrades.","k":[],"o":[["VPC CNI","EKS with security groups per Pod","IPs are scarce and you need eBPF features"],["Cilium","eBPF, L7 policy, Hubble, kube-proxy replacement","kernels are old or the platform does not allow it"],["Calico","BGP, mature policy model, mixed environments","you want built-in L7 and flow observability"]]}]},{"id":"s51-storage-deep-dive","title":"Storage deep dive","c":[{"id":"s51-storage-deep-dive-c1","t":"The CSI architecture","p":"The Container Storage Interface lets storage vendors ship a driver as Pods instead of code inside Kubernetes. A controller part talks to the storage API and a node part mounts volumes.","k":[["Which part mounts a volume on the node?","The CSI node plugin DaemonSet, called by the kubelet through the NodeStage and NodePublish calls."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s51-storage-deep-dive-c2","t":"StorageClasses, binding modes and reclaim","p":"A StorageClass is a template for dynamic volumes: which driver, which parameters, when to bind and what happens after deletion.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s51-storage-deep-dive-c3","t":"Volume expansion","p":"You can grow a PVC by editing its requested size. The driver resizes the disk, and the filesystem grows, often without restarting the Pod.","k":[["Can you shrink a PVC?","No. Kubernetes only supports growing. Restore into a smaller volume from a backup instead."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s51-storage-deep-dive-c4","t":"Snapshots and clones","p":"A VolumeSnapshot captures a volume at a point in time through the CSI driver. You can create a new PVC from it, or clone a PVC directly.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s51-storage-deep-dive-c5","t":"Topology and access modes","p":"Volumes live somewhere: a zone, a node or a rack. Topology rules keep Pods and data together. Access modes say who may mount at once.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s51-storage-deep-dive-c6","t":"Ephemeral volumes","p":"Some volumes live only as long as a Pod: emptyDir, ConfigMap and Secret volumes, and generic ephemeral volumes that use a real StorageClass.","k":[["What happens to a generic ephemeral volume when the Pod is deleted?","Its PVC is owned by the Pod and is deleted with it."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s51-storage-deep-dive-c7","t":"Backup and restore with Velero","p":"Velero backs up Kubernetes objects to object storage and can back up volume data through CSI snapshots or file-level copy (kopia).","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s52-advanced-scheduling","title":"Advanced scheduling","c":[{"id":"s52-advanced-scheduling-c1","t":"The scheduling cycle and plugins","p":"For each Pod the scheduler filters nodes that can run it, scores the rest, and binds the best. Every step is a plugin.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s52-advanced-scheduling-c2","t":"Scheduler profiles and configuration","p":"A KubeSchedulerConfiguration can define several profiles in one scheduler, each with its own name and plugin set. A Pod picks one with schedulerName.","k":[["How does a Pod pick a profile?","With spec.schedulerName set to the profile name."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s52-advanced-scheduling-c3","t":"Priority and preemption","p":"A PriorityClass gives Pods an integer priority. When a high priority Pod cannot fit, the scheduler may evict lower priority Pods to make room (preemption).","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s52-advanced-scheduling-c4","t":"Topology spread and affinity at scale","p":"topologySpreadConstraints keep replicas balanced across zones or nodes. Affinity and anti-affinity attract or repel Pods.","k":[["Why does imbalance appear after node failures even with spread rules?","Constraints are checked only when a Pod is scheduled. Existing Pods are not moved afterwards."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s52-advanced-scheduling-c5","t":"The descheduler","p":"The descheduler is a separate component that evicts Pods that are badly placed now, so the scheduler can place them again.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s52-advanced-scheduling-c6","t":"Kueue and job queueing","p":"Kueue is a Kubernetes SIG project that adds quotas and queues for batch workloads. Jobs are suspended until their quota is admitted.","k":[["Why is a Kueue Job created suspended?","Kueue unsuspends it only when quota is available, so Pods are not created before admission."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s53-stateful-databases-operators","title":"Stateful workloads, databases and operators","c":[{"id":"s53-stateful-databases-operators-c1","t":"Should this database run on Kubernetes","p":"Running state on Kubernetes is a trade-off between portability and operational effort. A managed service moves the work to the provider.","k":[],"o":[["Managed service","production data and a small team","you need a feature the service does not offer"],["Operator on Kubernetes","many instances, GitOps, portability","storage or backups are not proven"],["StatefulSet by hand","learning and throwaway tests","anything you would be paged for"]]},{"id":"s53-stateful-databases-operators-c2","t":"StatefulSet guarantees and limits","p":"A StatefulSet gives each replica a stable name, stable network identity and its own PVC. It starts and stops replicas in order.","k":[["What happens to PVCs when you scale a StatefulSet to zero?","They stay by default, so data survives and returns when you scale up, unless a retention policy says otherwise."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s53-stateful-databases-operators-c3","t":"The operator pattern for databases","p":"An operator is a controller plus CRDs that encodes what a human DBA does: create a cluster, fail over, back up, upgrade and rotate certificates.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s53-stateful-databases-operators-c4","t":"PostgreSQL with CloudNativePG","p":"CloudNativePG manages PostgreSQL clusters with one primary and streaming replicas, using native Kubernetes objects and no external HA tool.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s53-stateful-databases-operators-c5","t":"Kafka with Strimzi","p":"Strimzi runs Apache Kafka on Kubernetes with CRDs for clusters, topics, users and connectors.","k":[["Why set min.insync.replicas to 2 with replicas 3?","Writes with acks=all need two copies, so one broker can fail without losing data or availability."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s53-stateful-databases-operators-c6","t":"Backup, restore and disaster recovery","p":"Replication protects against node loss. Backups protect against mistakes and corruption. Both are needed.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s53-stateful-databases-operators-c7","t":"Operating stateful workloads safely","p":"Day-two work: upgrades, node drains, disk growth, monitoring and protecting data from accidental deletion.","k":[["Which risk does a PDB address?","Voluntary disruptions such as drains. It does not protect from node crashes or data deletion."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s54-batch-and-ml","title":"Batch and ML on Kubernetes","c":[{"id":"s54-batch-and-ml-c1","t":"Jobs, completions and failure handling","p":"A Job runs Pods until a number of them finish successfully. It retries failed Pods up to a limit.","k":[["Why use the Ignore action for DisruptionTarget?","Evictions and preemption are not application faults, so they should not consume the retry budget."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s54-batch-and-ml-c2","t":"Indexed Jobs and work queues","p":"Indexed completion mode gives every Pod a number in JOBCOMPLETIONINDEX. Each Pod processes its own slice of the data.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s54-batch-and-ml-c3","t":"CronJobs done properly","p":"A CronJob creates Jobs on a schedule. It has settings that control overlap, missed runs and history.","k":[["Which concurrencyPolicy avoids two overlapping runs?","Forbid skips a new run while the previous one is still active."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s54-batch-and-ml-c4","t":"GPUs and device plugins","p":"GPUs are exposed to Kubernetes as extended resources such as nvidia.com/gpu by a device plugin. Pods request them in limits.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s54-batch-and-ml-c5","t":"Distributed training and JobSet","p":"Training across many Pods needs them to start together and find each other. Operators and JobSet provide this.","k":[["What problem does gang scheduling solve?","Jobs that need all workers at once otherwise hold partial resources and may never start."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s54-batch-and-ml-c6","t":"Cost and capacity for batch","p":"Batch is the best candidate for spot or preemptible capacity, scale-to-zero node pools and bin-packing.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s55-security-hardening","title":"Security hardening","c":[{"id":"s55-security-hardening-c1","t":"CIS Benchmark and kube-bench","p":"The CIS Kubernetes Benchmark is a checklist of secure settings for the control plane, etcd, kubelet and policies. kube-bench runs the checks automatically.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s55-security-hardening-c2","t":"Node and kubelet hardening","p":"The kubelet exposes an API on each node. Closing it down and keeping nodes minimal limits what an attacker can do after reaching a node.","k":[["Why limit the IMDS hop limit to 1?","A Pod network adds a hop, so Pods cannot reach node credentials while the node itself still can."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s55-security-hardening-c3","t":"Seccomp and AppArmor","p":"Seccomp filters which Linux system calls a container may make. AppArmor restricts file, network and capability access by profile.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s55-security-hardening-c4","t":"Runtime security with Falco","p":"Falco watches system calls and Kubernetes audit events and raises alerts when behaviour matches a rule, such as a shell opened inside a container.","k":[["What does Falco add that admission policy does not?","Detection of what processes actually do after start, not only the Pod configuration at creation."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s55-security-hardening-c5","t":"Supply chain admission","p":"Admission policies can require that images come from approved registries, are signed, and have attestations such as an SBOM, before they may run.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s55-security-hardening-c6","t":"Encrypting Secrets at rest","p":"By default Secrets are only base64 encoded in etcd. Encryption at rest makes the API server encrypt them before writing.","k":[["Why keep identity last in the provider list?","It lets the server still read old unencrypted data during migration, but new writes use the first provider."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s55-security-hardening-c7","t":"Audit logging","p":"Audit logs record who did what to which object and when. The API server writes them according to an audit policy.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s56-kubeadm-bootstrap","title":"Bootstrapping with kubeadm, the hard way","c":[{"id":"s56-kubeadm-bootstrap-c1","t":"What a cluster is made of","p":"A cluster is a handful of programs plus certificates that let them trust one another: etcd, kube-apiserver, kube-controller-manager, kube-scheduler on the control plane and kubelet, container runtime and CNI on each node.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s56-kubeadm-bootstrap-c2","t":"Prepare nodes","p":"Before kubeadm runs, each machine needs a supported OS, a container runtime, the kubelet, kubeadm and kubectl, and a few kernel settings.","k":[["Which cgroup driver should containerd and kubelet share?","systemd on systemd-based hosts. A mismatch causes unstable Pods and kubelet errors."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s56-kubeadm-bootstrap-c3","t":"kubeadm init, phase by phase","p":"kubeadm init runs ordered phases: preflight checks, certificates, kubeconfigs, static Pod manifests, wait for the control plane, upload config, bootstrap tokens and add-ons.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s56-kubeadm-bootstrap-c4","t":"Certificates and PKI","p":"Components authenticate with X.509 client certificates signed by cluster CAs. kubeadm creates a cluster CA, an etcd CA and a front-proxy CA.","k":[["What happens when the API server certificate expires?","Clients and components fail TLS verification and the cluster appears down until certificates are renewed and Pods restarted."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s56-kubeadm-bootstrap-c5","t":"Joining workers and extra control planes","p":"Nodes join using a bootstrap token and the hash of the cluster CA. The kubelet then requests its own certificate.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s56-kubeadm-bootstrap-c6","t":"Install the CNI and verify","p":"After init, install a CNI so that Pods get IPs and CoreDNS can start. Then verify with a small workload.","k":[["Why are nodes NotReady right after init?","No CNI is installed yet, so the kubelet reports the network as not ready."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s56-kubeadm-bootstrap-c7","t":"What managed services do for you","p":"EKS and Gardener run and patch the control plane, etcd and certificates. You manage nodes (or let the service manage them), add-ons and workloads.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s57-ha-control-plane-etcd","title":"High availability control plane and etcd drills","c":[{"id":"s57-ha-control-plane-etcd-c1","t":"HA topologies","p":"A highly available control plane runs at least three API servers and three etcd members, behind one stable address.","k":[["Why use a load balancer address as controlPlaneEndpoint?","Nodes and users keep working when one API server fails or is replaced, and certificates stay valid."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s57-ha-control-plane-etcd-c2","t":"Raft quorum and failure math","p":"etcd uses the Raft algorithm. A write needs agreement from a majority (quorum) of members.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s57-ha-control-plane-etcd-c3","t":"Taking an etcd snapshot","p":"etcdctl snapshot save writes a consistent copy of the whole etcd database to a file.","k":[["Why encrypt etcd snapshots?","They contain all cluster data including Secrets in readable form unless encryption at rest was enabled."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s57-ha-control-plane-etcd-c4","t":"Restoring etcd, the drill","p":"Restore creates a new data directory from a snapshot. All members then start from the same state with new cluster identity.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s57-ha-control-plane-etcd-c5","t":"Failure drills","p":"A drill is a planned experiment: break one thing in a test cluster, observe, recover, and write down the steps and the time taken.","k":[["What happens to the API when two of three etcd members are down?","Quorum is lost. Reads may fail and writes are rejected until one member returns."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s57-ha-control-plane-etcd-c6","t":"etcd performance, defrag and monitoring","p":"etcd keeps history of changes, and its database file grows. Compaction removes old revisions and defragmentation returns space to the disk.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s58-cluster-api-fleet","title":"Cluster API and fleet management","c":[{"id":"s58-cluster-api-fleet-c1","t":"Why manage clusters declaratively","p":"Instead of scripts that create clusters, you describe each cluster in YAML in Git and a controller makes it real and keeps it that way.","k":[],"o":[["Cluster API","many clusters on varied infrastructure","one managed cluster is enough"],["Terraform pipeline","you already use it for the rest of the cloud","you need continuous drift correction"],["Gardener","many conformant clusters with a central team","you need a single small cluster"]]},{"id":"s58-cluster-api-fleet-c2","t":"Cluster API objects","p":"Cluster API (CAPI) is a Kubernetes SIG project. It defines CRDs such as Cluster, Machine, MachineDeployment and MachineSet, similar to Pod, Deployment and ReplicaSet but for nodes.","k":[["Which CAPI object replaces broken nodes automatically?","A MachineHealthCheck, which deletes machines that fail its conditions so the MachineSet creates new ones."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s58-cluster-api-fleet-c3","t":"Upgrades and node pools with CAPI","p":"You change the Kubernetes version or machine template in YAML. CAPI performs a rolling replacement of control plane then workers.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s58-cluster-api-fleet-c4","t":"ClusterClass and topology","p":"ClusterClass is a reusable blueprint. A Cluster then says \"use this class, with these variables\" instead of repeating every object.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s58-cluster-api-fleet-c5","t":"Fleet GitOps with Argo CD or Flux","p":"A fleet needs the same add-ons (ingress, monitoring, policy) on every cluster, with per-cluster differences. GitOps tools do this from one repository.","k":[["How does ApplicationSet know which clusters exist?","Argo CD cluster secrets are the inventory. The cluster generator creates one app per matching registered cluster."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s58-cluster-api-fleet-c6","t":"Crossplane and composition","p":"Crossplane turns cloud resources into Kubernetes objects and lets platform teams publish simple custom APIs built from them.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s58-cluster-api-fleet-c7","t":"Fleet policy, inventory and upgrades at scale","p":"At scale the questions change: what versions are running, which clusters are out of date, which violate policy.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s59-gardener-kyma-concepts","title":"Gardener and Kyma concepts","c":[{"id":"s59-gardener-kyma-concepts-c1","t":"Garden, seed and shoot","p":"Gardener is a Kubernetes extension that manages Kubernetes clusters at scale. It uses Kubernetes to run Kubernetes.","k":[["Where does a shoot's kube-apiserver run?","As a Pod in a namespace of the seed cluster, not on the shoot's own worker nodes."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s59-gardener-kyma-concepts-c2","t":"Reading a Shoot specification","p":"A Shoot object describes the cluster: provider, region, Kubernetes version, networking, worker pools, maintenance and hibernation.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s59-gardener-kyma-concepts-c3","t":"Maintenance, updates and hibernation","p":"Gardener updates clusters in a maintenance window and can hibernate them, which stops workers while keeping the cluster definition and data.","k":[["Why define PDBs for Gardener maintenance?","Node replacement drains nodes. PDBs limit how many replicas of a service are evicted at once."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s59-gardener-kyma-concepts-c4","t":"Gardener extensions and operations","p":"Gardener keeps cloud specifics out of its core. Extensions (provider, OS, network, DNS, certificates) handle each environment.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s59-gardener-kyma-concepts-c5","t":"Kyma runtime on top of Gardener","p":"SAP BTP, Kyma runtime provides a Gardener shoot already prepared with Kyma. You get a kubeconfig, a managed set of modules and BTP integration.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s59-gardener-kyma-concepts-c6","t":"The Kyma custom resource and release channels","p":"The Kyma resource in kyma-system lists enabled modules and their channel. Lifecycle Manager reads it and installs or upgrades the modules.","k":[["Where do you enable or disable a Kyma module?","In the Kyma custom resource (or the BTP cockpit, which edits it)."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s60-multicluster-federation","title":"Multi-cluster and federation","c":[{"id":"s60-multicluster-federation-c1","t":"Why more than one cluster","p":"Teams use several clusters for blast radius, regional placement, compliance boundaries, separate environments and independent upgrades.","k":[],"o":[["Namespace plus policy","trusted teams in one company","tenants are hostile or regulated differently"],["Virtual cluster","teams need their own API and CRDs","you need hardware isolation"],["Separate cluster","compliance, region or blast radius","cost and operations are the main constraint"]]},{"id":"s60-multicluster-federation-c2","t":"Cross-cluster service discovery","p":"By default a Service name resolves only inside its cluster. Multi-cluster discovery makes a service in another cluster reachable by name.","k":[["What must not overlap in a flat multi-cluster network?","Pod and often Service CIDRs, since addresses must be unique across clusters."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s60-multicluster-federation-c3","t":"Cilium Cluster Mesh and Submariner","p":"Both connect the Pod networks of several clusters so that Pods in different clusters reach each other directly.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s60-multicluster-federation-c4","t":"Workload placement with Karmada and OCM","p":"Karmada and Open Cluster Management (OCM) offer a control plane that spreads workloads across member clusters by policy.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s60-multicluster-federation-c5","t":"Global traffic and failover","p":"Users reach the nearest healthy cluster through DNS or an anycast/global load balancer that checks cluster health.","k":[["Why is data harder than traffic in multi-region failover?","Traffic can be redirected quickly, but writes must be replicated and consistent, and promotion of a replica risks data loss or split brain."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s60-multicluster-federation-c6","t":"Identity, secrets and policy across clusters","p":"Several clusters need consistent identity for people, workloads and secrets without copying long-lived credentials.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s60-multicluster-federation-c7","t":"Operating a multi-cluster setup","p":"Day-two work includes keeping versions aligned, monitoring all clusters in one place and writing runbooks that name the cluster.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s61-cka-ckad-labs","title":"CKA and CKAD exam labs","c":[{"id":"s61-cka-ckad-labs-c1","t":"Exam setup and speed routine","p":"Spend the first minute preparing the shell, then work from imperative commands that generate YAML.","k":[["Why use --dry-run=client -o yaml?","It generates a correct manifest skeleton without creating anything, so you edit instead of typing from scratch."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s61-cka-ckad-labs-c2","t":"Lab 1, workloads and rollouts (10 minutes)","p":"Create, scale, update and roll back a Deployment, and expose it. A core skill for both exams.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s61-cka-ckad-labs-c3","t":"Lab 2, config, secrets and probes (10 minutes)","p":"Inject configuration from ConfigMaps and Secrets and add health probes.","k":[["What is the difference between envFrom and env with valueFrom?","envFrom imports all keys of a source as variables; valueFrom picks a single key and lets you rename it."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s61-cka-ckad-labs-c4","t":"Lab 3, scheduling and node tasks (10 minutes)","p":"Control where Pods run with labels, taints, tolerations and affinity, and take a node out of service.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s61-cka-ckad-labs-c5","t":"Lab 4, networking and Services (10 minutes)","p":"Connect Pods through Services, restrict access with NetworkPolicy, and publish through Ingress or Gateway.","k":[["Does a NetworkPolicy do anything without a CNI that supports it?","No. The object is accepted but ignored unless the CNI enforces NetworkPolicy."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s61-cka-ckad-labs-c6","t":"Lab 5, storage and RBAC (10 minutes)","p":"Give a Pod persistent storage and give a ServiceAccount limited permissions.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s61-cka-ckad-labs-c7","t":"Lab 6, cluster tasks for CKA (15 minutes)","p":"Administrator tasks: etcd backup, kubeadm upgrade, troubleshooting a NotReady node and static Pods.","k":[["Where do you put a static Pod manifest?","In the directory configured as staticPodPath in the kubelet config, normally /etc/kubernetes/manifests."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s62-cks-labs","title":"CKS exam labs","c":[{"id":"s62-cks-labs-c1","t":"Lab 1, Pod security and securityContext (8 minutes)","p":"Make a Pod meet the restricted Pod Security Standard and enforce it on a namespace.","k":[["Which Pod Security level requires seccomp RuntimeDefault?","restricted. Baseline allows unset seccomp but forbids unconfined."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s62-cks-labs-c2","t":"Lab 2, RBAC least privilege (8 minutes)","p":"Find and remove over-broad permissions and prove the result with can-i.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s62-cks-labs-c3","t":"Lab 3, NetworkPolicy default deny (8 minutes)","p":"Isolate a namespace with default deny, then open only the flows you need, including DNS.","k":[["Why must DNS be allowed explicitly after default deny?","Egress deny also blocks the Pod's DNS lookups to CoreDNS, so name resolution breaks."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s62-cks-labs-c4","t":"Lab 4, image and supply chain checks (8 minutes)","p":"Scan an image, restrict registries by admission and verify a signature.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s62-cks-labs-c5","t":"Lab 5, runtime detection and Falco (8 minutes)","p":"Detect suspicious behaviour in a running container and trace it to a Pod.","k":[["Why isolate before deleting a compromised Pod?","Deleting destroys evidence. A deny policy stops further damage while you collect logs and the Pod definition."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s62-cks-labs-c6","t":"Lab 6, audit logs and secrets (8 minutes)","p":"Write an audit policy, read audit events, and check that Secrets are encrypted at rest.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s63-troubleshooting-scenarios-a","title":"Troubleshooting scenarios 1 to 7, networking and storage","c":[{"id":"s63-troubleshooting-scenarios-a-c1","t":"Scenario 1, Service has no endpoints","p":"The Service exists but requests time out or are refused. The Service routes only to Pods that match its selector and are Ready.","k":[["Which Pods appear in endpoints?","Pods matching the selector whose Ready condition is true (unless publishNotReadyAddresses is set)."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s63-troubleshooting-scenarios-a-c2","t":"Scenario 2, DNS failures","p":"Pods cannot resolve names, or resolve slowly. CoreDNS answers cluster names and forwards the rest.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s63-troubleshooting-scenarios-a-c3","t":"Scenario 3, Ingress or Gateway returns 502 or 503","p":"The load balancer works but users see 502, 503 or 404. The proxy cannot find or reach a healthy backend.","k":[["What does 503 with \"no healthy upstream\" usually mean?","The proxy has no ready endpoints for the route's backend."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s63-troubleshooting-scenarios-a-c4","t":"Scenario 4, NetworkPolicy blocks traffic","p":"Traffic worked yesterday and now times out after a policy was added. Policies are additive allow lists once a Pod is selected.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s63-troubleshooting-scenarios-a-c5","t":"Scenario 5, PVC stuck in Pending","p":"A PersistentVolumeClaim never binds, so the Pod stays Pending.","k":[["Is a Pending PVC with WaitForFirstConsumer an error?","Not by itself. It waits for a Pod to be scheduled so that the volume can be created in the right zone."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s63-troubleshooting-scenarios-a-c6","t":"Scenario 6, Multi-Attach error after a node failure","p":"A Pod using a ReadWriteOnce volume is rescheduled to another node and cannot start because the volume is still attached to the old node.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s63-troubleshooting-scenarios-a-c7","t":"Scenario 7, Node NotReady","p":"A node stops reporting Ready. The control plane marks its Pods for eviction after the toleration period (5 minutes by default).","k":[["After you finish node maintenance, what must you check?","That the node is uncordoned and schedulable, because reconciliation or a partial drain can leave it cordoned."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s64-troubleshooting-scenarios-b","title":"Troubleshooting scenarios 8 to 14, rollouts, policies and access","c":[{"id":"s64-troubleshooting-scenarios-b-c1","t":"Scenario 8, Rollout stuck","p":"A Deployment update never completes. The new ReplicaSet does not become available within progressDeadlineSeconds.","k":[["What does ProgressDeadlineExceeded mean?","The rollout made no progress within the deadline. The Deployment reports it; it does not roll back by itself."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s64-troubleshooting-scenarios-b-c2","t":"Scenario 9, A webhook blocks all changes","p":"Creating or updating objects fails with failed calling webhook. An admission webhook is unreachable and set to failurePolicy: Fail.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s64-troubleshooting-scenarios-b-c3","t":"Scenario 10, Namespace stuck Terminating","p":"A namespace deletion never finishes. Remaining objects have finalizers, or an API service is unavailable so the controller cannot list all resources.","k":[["Why is clearing a finalizer a last resort?","The finalizer exists to clean external resources. Removing it skips that clean-up and can leave cloud resources behind."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s64-troubleshooting-scenarios-b-c4","t":"Scenario 11, HPA shows unknown metrics","p":"The Horizontal Pod Autoscaler reports <unknown>/80% and does not scale.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s64-troubleshooting-scenarios-b-c5","t":"Scenario 12, CronJob does not run","p":"A CronJob exists but no Jobs appear, or Jobs appear but fail.","k":[["How can you test a CronJob without waiting for the schedule?","Create a Job from it with kubectl create job --from=cronjob/NAME."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s64-troubleshooting-scenarios-b-c6","t":"Scenario 13, Forbidden by RBAC","p":"A user, pipeline or controller receives forbidden: User \"x\" cannot list resource \"pods\".","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s64-troubleshooting-scenarios-b-c7","t":"Scenario 14, Expired or unissued certificates","p":"Browsers show certificate errors, or a Certificate stays Ready=False in cert-manager.","k":[["What is the order of cert-manager objects during ACME issuance?","Certificate, CertificateRequest, Order, Challenge. Failures show in the lowest object that exists."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s65-troubleshooting-scenarios-c","title":"Troubleshooting scenarios 15 to 20, platform and cloud","c":[{"id":"s65-troubleshooting-scenarios-c-c1","t":"Scenario 15, API server slow or returning 429","p":"kubectl is slow, controllers log timeouts, and some requests return 429 Too Many Requests.","k":[["Which setting makes a LIST cheaper for the API server?","Reading from the watch cache (resourceVersion=0 or informers) and using pagination, instead of repeated full quorum reads."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s65-troubleshooting-scenarios-c-c2","t":"Scenario 16, etcd database space exceeded","p":"Writes fail with mvcc: database space exceeded. etcd raised a NOSPACE alarm and became read-only.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s65-troubleshooting-scenarios-c-c3","t":"Scenario 17, Kyma APIRule returns 403 or 404","p":"An APIRule exposes a service through the Kyma API Gateway, but calls return 403, 404 or a redirect to login.","k":[["Why must the workload namespace have Istio injection for JWT rules to apply?","Authorization is enforced by the Envoy sidecar. Without it, the policy objects cannot protect or identify the workload."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s65-troubleshooting-scenarios-c-c4","t":"Scenario 18, EKS IRSA or Pod Identity AccessDenied","p":"A Pod calls AWS and gets AccessDenied, or uses the node role instead of its own role.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s65-troubleshooting-scenarios-c-c5","t":"Scenario 19, Image pull rate limits and registry failures","p":"Many Pods fail with toomanyrequests or 429 when pulling from a public registry, especially after a scale-out or node replacement.","k":[["Why do failures burst after a scale-out?","New nodes have empty image caches, so every node pulls at once from the same source and hits the limit."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s65-troubleshooting-scenarios-c-c6","t":"Scenario 20, Gardener maintenance stuck on a drain","p":"A shoot reconcile or node roll stays in Processing because a node cannot be drained.","k":[],"o":[]}]}]};